docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:9:The window loop alone consumes 77,333 CPU-s = 21.48 CPU-h—2.94× diaTracer’s entire 7.3 CPU-h. Even deleting every other speXtract phase would not produce CPU parity. Scheduler improvements can plausibly remove 15–25% wall time. Closing the remaining gap requires reducing the number of fragment traces and/or precursor–fragment correlation work.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:11:There is also a revision mismatch. The measured run is clearly from the parent of commit `d0b3cb4`. The current file has already replaced the rigid 8×12 nested grid with one 100-thread task pool, parallelized `FragGrid`, changed the admission multiplier from 10× to 16×, and shortened the wait from 200 to 20 ms. See [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2322](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2322), [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2344](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2344), and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363). The supplied measurements therefore do not characterize the current source.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:17:The per-window stage timers are elapsed stage durations summed across windows—not CPU-seconds, despite the incorrect log message at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2671](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2671). The timers surround stages with `steady_clock`, for example [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2415](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2415) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2472](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2472).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:23:- Average cores used per active window:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:33:| Known serial stages | at least 4.06 | Materialize + old serial `fraggrid` + emit = 420.9 of 8139.5 window-s, using one rather than 12 cores |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:36:The serial-stage estimate is only a lower bound: the `trace` timer also includes serial band construction, sorting, canonicalization and result merging surrounding the parallel detector.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:40:- **Eight-window cap:** not the primary limit. Eight windows × twelve threads already exposes 96 threads. The four permanently unused threads cost at most 4.2%. The rigid topology is bad chiefly because idle teams cannot help surviving windows, which the current task-pool rewrite explicitly addresses at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:42:- **Across-window imbalance/admission:** measurable but bounded. Perfectly filling eight slots reduces the loop from 1139 to \(8139.5/8 = 1017.4\) s: at most 121.6 s, or 9.0% of total wall time. Because admission waiting is outside the stage timers, the supplied data cannot separate window-size tail from memory admission.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:45:  \((395.8-395.8/12)/8 ≈ 45\) wall-s, about 3.3% of total runtime. The current implementation now uses a task loop at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:741](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:741).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:47:- **Inside-band/precursor imbalance:** real. After accounting for outer vacancy and the explicitly serial stages, roughly 13.8 of the missing 28.1 cores remain. Quantile bands balance sampled peak count, not `MassTraceDetection` work; its candidate work is locally superlinear in peak density. The partition and detector are at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:968](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:968) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1015](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1015). Scoring also uses chunks of 32 precursors whose costs vary with IM density, support length and emitted-fragment count; current equivalent is [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2560](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2560).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:49:- **Raw trace/correlation volume:** the actual binding problem. Trace and score occupy 94.83% of summed active window time. The scoring path takes an IM slice, performs the RT gate, then walks every surviving fragment’s CSR support for the dot product at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1534](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1534). With up to 8.5 M fragment traces per window, this is an enormous intermediate workload unrelated to the final count of 863 k spectra.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:56:- Exactly eight window workers and real 12-thread nested teams were used.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:58:- Admission did not regularly restrict execution below eight windows.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:59:- The timers measure elapsed per-window stage duration—as the source shows—rather than CPU time as the log incorrectly claims.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:61:The settling measurement is cheap: record, per window and band, admission-wait time, stage start/end, thread CPU time and active-team size. One OMPT trace or even four atomic histograms would separate admission, window tail, band imbalance and memory stalls.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:67:During one old-revision window, memory includes:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:80:That is approximately \(32Q\) bytes plus trace headers. A few hundred million support points therefore explain a 10–14 GB transient without invoking a mysterious full-window copy.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:84:But “97 GB / 8 = 12 GB per window” is not a valid exact measurement. While the loop runs:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:92:\(0.70 × 863319 × 500 × 16 ≈ 4.83\) GB, excluding vector capacity and metadata. The 87.9 GB end-of-loop RSS proves that a substantial amount is persistent output or retained allocator memory, not eight live windows.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:98:Average compact bytes per window are \(20.849/24 = 0.869\) GB. The old estimate therefore booked approximately:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:112:A 5.0× speedup on twelve nominal workers corresponds, under a simple Amdahl model, to an effective serial fraction of:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:118:That is credible because only the actual band detector and valley-split chunks are parallel. The following remain serial or effectively serial inside `detectTraces_`:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:138:Equal output counts do not imply equal work. Runtime is governed by raw points, detected features and candidate pairs, not the final number of spectra.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:144:2. **A specialized feature detector versus generic greedy MTD.** speXtract invokes OpenMS `MassTraceDetection` separately over haloed bands at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:919](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:919). The source itself records that increasing bands from 5 to 12 to 24 reduced trace CPU from 11,168 to 5,378 to 2,972 s because MTD candidate scanning is superlinear in band population at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2426](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2426). That is an algorithmic red flag, not a mere compiler issue.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:146:3. **Fewer precursor hypotheses.** diaTracer isotope-groups MS1 signals and discards peaks not belonging to a cluster. speXtract assigns unsupported singleton traces a default charge unless an optional gate is enabled at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2096](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2096). More precursor hypotheses mean proportionally more fragment scans and correlations.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:150:5. **Brute-force candidate association.** speXtract indexes only by IM, then linearly scans that slice and tests RT before correlation at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515). A joint RT–IM feature index or feature-range intersection can avoid most candidate visits. The public diaTracer paper does not disclose enough implementation detail to prove that it does so, but its retained m/z/IM signal ranges make it possible.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:152:6. **Correlation support representation.** speXtract constructs a global ~1,300-point RT axis for every window, stores every fragment support, and repeatedly streams that support for every eligible precursor. diaTracer correlates bounded precursor and fragment XIC features after feature segmentation. The paper does not publish sufficient low-level detail to quantify this difference, but it is a plausible major work reducer.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:160:| 1 | Rebenchmark the current unified task pool: window tasks, band tasks and precursor tasks share one 100-thread team ([loop](//Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2373)). | Expected 15–20% total wall reduction; hard ceiling 24.5% from 67.9→96 loop speedup. | Medium runtime/scheduling risk; intended output-neutral because writes use indexed slots. | Run S08 with per-window digests and peptide-set equality; capture active-thread timeline. If mean loop use remains below ~80 cores, it failed. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:161:| 2 | Retain the current `buildFragGrid` rewrite: avoid the all-support RT temporary, parallel-fill CSR and consume XICs in place ([implementation](//Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:716)). | Approximately 3–4% total wall; potentially 2–4 GB less transient memory per heavy window. | Low–medium; output-neutral only if RT set and per-fragment arithmetic remain identical. | Compare per-window trace/grid digests and correlations near the 0.3 cutoff; log RSS before and after grid construction. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:162:| 3 | Test 24 trace bands under the task pool instead of the old rigid grid. | Source’s own S30 data show 44.7% less trace CPU versus 12 bands; weighted ceiling is about 18% total wall, realistic 10–18%. | Medium; intended neutral, but finite halos and greedy MTD can make boundary behavior differ. Narrow-window halo memory may grow sharply. | Run the two heaviest and two narrowest windows at 12/24 bands; require identical trace digest and emitted spectra before a full run. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:163:| 4 | Replace the IM-only candidate scan with an exact joint RT–IM bin/posting index; preserve fragment order for correlation arithmetic. | Likely 4–10% total; absolute ceiling is the 38.8% of total wall represented by score. | Medium; output-neutral if boundary comparisons and summation order are preserved. | Use existing `stat_slice_ / stat_rtpass_` logging at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2684](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2684). If scanned/passed is below ~2×, abandon it. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:164:| 5 | Tile scoring fragment-major or by RT–IM cell so fragment CSR remains cache-hot across nearby precursors. | Roughly 10–25% of score time, or 4–10% total wall. | Medium–high; reordering floating-point sums can move borderline correlations and therefore change output. | Prototype on one heavy window; compare pair counts, hardware cache misses and all correlations within ±1e-5 of threshold. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:165:| 6 | Eliminate full band `PeakMap` copies using immutable spectrum/range views or one compact per-band index. | Perhaps 2–5% wall and 1–2 GB less transient memory per average window. | High implementation risk around OpenMS ownership; output-neutral in principle. | Instrument bytes copied during partition. If partition/copy CPU is below 2% and RSS is not peaking there, reject it. |
