docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:1:• I've read `src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp` and the OpenMS `MassTraceDetection` source it calls (checkout at `~/Claude/OpenMS`, `src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp` — the patched tree this project builds against). Verdict up front: your hypothesis is **right, and understated**. There are at least three mechanisms you didn't list, one of which falsifies the help text's premise before your mechanism 1 even gets started.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:9:  - **Running-centroid acceptance (your mechanism 2).** Confirmed exactly as you describe. The centroid is seeded from the apex (`MassTraceDetection.cpp:508-512`) and updated by intensity-weighted mean after *every* accepted peak (`processPeak_`, line 418). Acceptance is `candidate.mz ∈ centroid ± 3·ftl_sd` (`isPeakAcceptable_`, lines 355-356, 365-368). The walk is real.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:15:  **(i) The help text's premise is off by 3× before drift is considered.** "Traces cannot span more than trace:mass_error_ppm in m/z" is wrong even for a single accepted peak: `ftl_sd` is initialised as `centroid_mz/1e6 · mass_error_ppm` (line 537) and the acceptance window is **±3·ftl_sd** (lines 301-302, 355-356). One step spans 3× ppm, not 1×. So the "20× halo" is really a 6.7-step-width halo, not a 20-step one.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:17:  **(ii) `reestimate_mt_sd` defaults to `"true"` (line 34) and speXtract never overrides it** (no `reestimate` or `trace_termination` anywhere in `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp`). Every accepted peak re-estimates `ftl_sd` from the gathered peaks via `updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust` (lines 178-191, called at 440), and that update has **no cap tying it back to `mass_error_ppm`**. So the per-step acceptance width is itself a function of which peaks the band gave the trace. This is simultaneously a strengthening of your mechanism 2 (span bound is not just unbounded, it's not even expressible in ppm) and an independent instance of mechanism 3 (the window width differs between band counts on identical underlying data).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:19:  **(iii) Global apex-order contention via `peak_visited`.** `run_` processes apices in *decreasing intensity* across the whole map (line 494 iterates `chrom_apices.crbegin()`) and claims peaks into one global `boost::dynamic_bitset peak_visited` (line 477); a visited peak is rejected for all later traces (lines 382-387). Banding forks that bitset per band (`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1077-1081` — each band runs its own `MassTraceDetection`). Two apices in different bands whose traces reach the same halo-region peak are mutually exclusive in the unbanded run (higher-intensity apex wins) but **both claim it** in the banded run. This is the strongest of all the channels: it needs no drift, no pathological input, and fires entirely *inside* the halo, so **no halo width can fix it** — widening the halo enlarges the contested region.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:21:  One correction to your framing, in the hostile spirit: you say `min_sample_rate` "terminates extension when occupancy drops." It doesn't in this configuration. Termination-by-sample-rate only happens in `trace_termination_criterion="sample_rate"` mode (lines 581-593, 635-647); the default is `"outlier"` (line 41; 5 consecutive misses, line 43), which speXtract never changes. `min_sample_rate` is here only a *post-hoc validity filter* in `isTraceValid_` (lines 462-468). The comment at `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:987-990` is wrong about the code path actually taken. This doesn't weaken your case — a validity flip is still a band-dependent deletion — but get the mechanism right in the writeup.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:25:  *Example 1 — mechanism 2 (drift defeats any fixed multiple).* ppm = 20, apex at scan 50, m/z 500.000, intensity 1000. Initial `ftl_sd` = 0.010, window ±0.030. At scans 51-61, place one peak per scan at `centroid + 0.0295`, with intensity doubling each scan (2000, 4000, …). Each peak is inside its ±0.030 window (the σ re-estimation only widens it further as the walk spreads the points). The centroid moves ≈0.0197 per step; after 11 steps it sits ≈500.19-500.21, i.e. **past the 20× halo half-width of 0.200** (`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1048-1049`). Put a band edge at 500.200 and the unbanded run keeps growing this trace while the banded run loses the later peaks — different trace, different centroid, possibly different core ownership. With constant instead of doubling intensity the walk is ≈0.0295·ln L, needing ~2400 scans to cross — slower, still unbounded. The point: **the span is not bounded by any multiple of ppm; 20 is a magic number and some magic numbers lose.**
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:27:  *Example 2 — mechanism 3(iii) (contention, fires inside the halo).* ppm = 20, edge at 500.100, halo ±0.200 (band A sees up to 500.300, band B down to 499.900). Spectrum at scan 101 contains one IM-eligible peak p at 500.110, intensity 5e5. Apex a (intensity 1e6) at 500.090, scan 100 → T_A's window covers p. Apex b (intensity 9e5) at 500.120, scan 102 → T_B extends down and its window covers p. **Unbanded:** a is processed first (higher intensity), claims p; when b's trace reaches scan 101, p is visited → miss → T_B has a gap, lower sample rate, different centroid. **Banded:** band B's private bitset lets T_B claim p; band A's run *also* claims p for T_A. Output: T_B differs in membership, centroid, and validity between N=1 and N=12 — and if T_A's banded centroid lands at 500.0999 while T_B's lands at 500.1001, the core filter at `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1083-1088` keeps **both**, emitting a duplicated near-edge trace where the unbanded run had one. Note the core filter dedups only when both versions' centroids land in the *same* core; banding-induced centroid shifts near an edge defeat it.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:29:  *Example 3 — mechanism 3 via validity flip.* Extend Example 2 so T_B visits 6 scans with 3 hits banded (rate 0.5, passes `isTraceValid_`, line 468) versus 2 hits unbanded (rate 0.33, rejected). A band boundary has now created/annihilated a trace wholesale. Valley splitting (`ElutionPeakDetection`, called at `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1000-1011`) then amplifies whatever membership differences exist, since split points are computed from the gathered intensity profile.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:37:  **(b) Gap partitioning — conditionally exact, and this is the one worth building.** Cut only at gaps in the m/z distribution. Then ship a **runtime exactness certificate**: after tracing, verify for every trace that its inflated extent `[min_member_mz − 3σ, max_member_mz + 3σ]` does not cross any cut. If no trace's reach crosses a cut, no two traces in different bands can share a peak, the `peak_visited` interactions are disjoint, apex ordering across bands is irrelevant, and the banded output is *provably* identical to the unbanded run. Cost: cuts won't coincide with equal work, so you need bin-packing of gap-delimited segments into balanced bands; and in dense windows there may be no usable gap at all, forcing fewer bands (or N=1) there. Note the data may cooperate — your own comment at `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1024-1025` says density varies by orders of magnitude, i.e. sparse regions exist. Verification: the certificate is cheap (one pass over output traces per window) and can be logged next to the existing span instrumentation at `:1139-1155`. Bonus: gap-derived band counts are **data-derived, not machine-derived**, which incidentally fixes your question-3 problem for this scheme.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:39:  **(c) Whole-window detection, parallelise elsewhere — exact by construction.** N=1 *is* the reference semantics. Cost is real and measured by you: trace CPU rises superlinearly with band size (your own numbers at `:2505-2507`: 2,972 → 11,168 s going 24 → 5 bands; N=1 is worse), and window-level parallelism caps at 24. Mitigations inside the rules: heaviest-first scheduling and cross-window task stealing already exist (`:2440-2452`); push parallelism into the other ~51% of the loop. Verification: none needed.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:47:  **"auto" should not exist.** It resolves to `ceil(n_threads / n_windows)` (`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2392-2393`): 5 bands on a 100-core box, 10 on a 224-core box. Since N changes the trace set (measured), auto makes the accepted-peptide set a function of the machine the run happened to land on. For a tool whose results are gated on peptide-set equality between builds, that is not a performance feature; it is a reproducibility bug with a help text. The comment at `:2338` ("canonically sorted, so output is thread-count-independent") is also false and should be corrected in the same pass — sorting fixes order nondeterminism, not set dependence on N.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:49:  Default: pin a constant (12 is as good as any — it's what your measurements were taken at) and treat it as part of the **method definition**, documented alongside `trace:mass_error_ppm`, with release notes when it ever changes. Same treatment for `perf:ms1_trace_bands` (`:908`), which has the identical defect and whose help text copies the same false exactness claim. Remove 0=auto from both, or make 0 a hard error. And fix the three false comments (`:909`, `:1020-1021`, `:2380-2381`).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:58:  2. **Trace level, per window.** Match traces between the two runs (centroid within 3σ, RT-overlap ≥ 50%); report unmatched-fraction each way, the centroid Δppm distribution, and the member-set-identity fraction. This attributes error to windows (wide vs narrow tiles will differ).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:59:  3. **Zero-cost incidence instrumentation, every run.** Extend the `span_log` block (`:1139-1155`) with per-window **m/z** extents: max trace extent vs the halo width (mechanism-2 incidence) and the count of traces whose ±3σ-inflated extent straddles a band edge (an upper bound on mechanism-3 exposure). Then every production run reports its own exposure instead of claiming exactness.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:60:  4. **Cheap continuous guard.** You already emit per-window trace digests (`:2524`) and a precursor digest (`:2165`). On a small file where N=1 is cheap, wire "N=1 vs N=12 digest equality" into CI as the regression tripwire; if they diverge after a code change, you learn immediately rather than at the next 18-minute reference run.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:66:  Here is the uncomfortable part, stated plainly: **your banding speedup is not only parallelism — it is algorithmic work reduction, and the work it skips is exactly the work that made the output correct.** The measured CPU fall with more bands (`:2505-2507`) comes from giving each `MassTraceDetection` call fewer candidate peaks per apex; changing the candidate set is precisely what changes the output. So "keep the bands' cost profile and the unbanded semantics" is not available without rewriting OpenMS, which is off the table.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:70:  1. **Gap-partition with certificate (2b).** Keeps most of the parallelism, eliminates the approximation *where the certificate holds*, and is machine-independent. Prerequisite measurement: the distribution of m/z gaps per window vs the p99 trace m/z extent (which 4.3 gives you). If dense windows lack usable gaps, they run with fewer bands and a logged certificate failure mode, and you know it from the log rather than from a digest diff three releases later.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.md:71:  2. **Window-level parallelism only (N=1), buy back the tail.** With the task pool, the 24-window ceiling mostly hurts at the end of the run; schedule heaviest-first (you do) and let the tail's idle cores absorb the MS1 pass and EPD chunks. Pay the ~2-4× trace CPU as the honest price of exactness.
docs/reviews/backlog-2026-09-02-kimi.md:3:  **1. B2 — the stated exclusions are wrong, and the decisive experiment was cancelled (blocker).** Row B2 (line 26) claims "MS1 arrives before all MS2 so flush groups are batch-independent" and "picker is `const`/stateless". Both fail in code. `SPEXTRACT_PICK_BATCH` sets one shared `kBatch` used by *both* buffers (`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:561` MS1, `:604` MS2, `:623-624`), so batch 64-vs-256 re-partitions the **MS1** parallel-pick groups too — MS1 flush groups are batch-*dependent*, and MS1 feeds MassTraceDetection → precursor inference → every downstream spectrum. And the picker exclusion is asserted, not shown: `flush_`/`flushMS1_` use `firstprivate(picker)` (`:525`, `:571`), so each thread *reuses one picker instance across many spectra*; any cross-call state makes output depend on thread→spectrum assignment, which is exactly what batch size changes. The history-vs-race probe built for this (`SPEXTRACT_PICK_STATIC`, `:522`; det4 in RUNTIME-PLAN) was cancelled when det2 passed **at fixed batch** — the one case where it can't see the effect. Correction: delete the two exclusions; add to the test plan (i) single-threaded 64-vs-256 (digests differ → loader/buffering logic; identical → scheduling/history), (ii) revive det4 static-vs-dynamic at batch 256, (iii) read the `SPEXTRACT_DET` stage digests at the **MS1-trace** stage first, not just MS2 windows.
docs/reviews/backlog-2026-09-02-kimi.md:7:  **3. D1 — the two-point transform is the measured m/z defect, but the −11.7% attribution is not isolated (major).** Confounds the row ignores: (a) the 1/K0 axis differs by construction ("converter-recalibrated 1/K0 vs our rational TIMS calibration", RUNTIME-PLAN line 219), and every IM-denominated gate — `pickIMCluster:im_tolerance_cluster` (`:1694`), `charge:iso_im_tolerance`, `im_weight_sigma` — shifts with it; (b) the reader matches windows by nearest `selected_ion_mz` within 0.05 Da (`MzPeakStreamLoad.h:141-142`) under an m/z axis bowed ~7 ppm — near-boundary windows can mis-match; (c) +2,693 spectra (925,595 vs 922,902) is unexplained in any doc. The D4 workaround dropping the per-window IM band restriction also admits off-band interferents into the corr gates. Correction: root cause → "two-point m/z proven (+7 ppm bow); peptide-loss attribution pending; IM axis uncontrolled." Pre-register step 8's falsifier: exact-calibration mzPeak must recover ≈12,217 peptides with high set overlap vs d2_P1; if it doesn't, the IM axis is implicated.
docs/reviews/backlog-2026-09-02-kimi.md:15:  **7. A1 — arithmetic checks; presentation overreaches (minor).** 444.7 − 4.4 − 11.4 ≈ 425 s and 22:22 − ~7 min ≈ 15:30 are consistent with RUNTIME-PLAN's tables. But the 425 s is attribution-by-subtraction, not a timer; the "~30 s" assumes decode + FrameCentroider + pick all parallelise at MS2-like efficiency, with no pre-registered range like the MS2 sweep's reading key (RUNTIME-PLAN lines 66-69) — Week-1 step 1 should carry one. Also "4% of frames" is wrong twice over: 1,343/17,448 = 7.7% of frames; 4% is of window-spectra — an unfortunate slip in a document whose first "recurring root cause" is ranking by the wrong denominator. Note `flushMS1_` parallel pick is already in the tree (`:564-591`); only loader-side MS1 decode is queued — the row's "built and queued" conflates the two.
