docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:5:  The window loop is 84.1% of wall, so everything else is rounding. Decompose its 67.9x against the intended 96-thread grid (:2297 `n_conc = n_threads/n_bands` = 8; :1000 band team = 12; :2519 score team `p_team` = 12; :2302 two active levels). Capacity = 96 × 1,139 = 109,344 thread-s; consumed = 77,333; idle = 32,011 s = **29.3%**. The decomposition is forced by the arithmetic:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:8:  - **Admission waits + loop tail: ~972 outer-thread-s ≈ 11,700 s of capacity (10.7%).** Outer busy = stage wall sums = 8,139.5 s; outer capacity = 8 × 1,139 = 9,112 s. The gap is threads parked in the 200 ms admit poll (:2340-2352) plus the ramp/tail where fewer than 8 of the 24 skewed windows remain. Heavy-first (:2253) already minimises this; the residual is the price of 24 work units on 8 slots with a 136x size spread. Each parked outer thread idles its whole 12-thread team, hence ×12.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:9:  - **Serial stages inside the loop: ~4,600 s (4.2%).** `materialize` (24.1 s) and `fraggrid` (395.8 s) run on the single outer worker (:2356, :2424 — `buildFragGrid` :714-744 has no OpenMP at all) while 11 team threads sit out: 420.9 window-wall-s × 11.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:11:  **Binding constraint: the raw work volume of trace + score (94.8% of window-time), not the concurrency cap.** Proof: even at 100% fill the loop wall is CPU/96 = 806 s, giving total ≈ 1,018 s — still 1.74x diaTracer's 586 s. Scheduling recovers at most ~29%; it cannot close a 2.3x wall gap.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:13:  **Contradiction you should know about:** the comment at :2274-2276 claims trace = 69.34% / score = 28.81% of window-loop *CPU*. On your run that is impossible — 0.693 × 77,333 = 53.6k CPU-s against a hard ceiling of 12 × 3,965.6 = 47.6k thread-s for the trace stage. The 69/29 split (measured on S30/S23) does not describe S08; score is far heavier here than the source believes. Your stage timers (:2355-2434, :2517-2534) measure *wall* per window, so **trace-vs-score CPU cannot be attributed from your numbers at all** — that is the missing measurement.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:15:  For my explanation to fail, one of these must be wrong: (a) the stage sums really are per-window wall (they are chrono deltas around each stage, :2366/:2423/:2426/:2531 — sound); (b) the 972-s outer gap is *scheduling*, not memory-forced serialization — settle by timing the `for(;;)` admit loop per window (two chrono reads around :2341-2352); (c) inner "idle" is idle, not memory-bandwidth stall — settle with `perf stat` on a score-heavy window, or run 1 window alone and see if fill rises.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:19:  ~12 GB per in-flight window is **explicable by the data structures, and ~30-40% of the transient is copies that need not coexist**. Per average window (compact 0.87 GB = 20,849 MB/24, at 10 B/peak):
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:30:  The transient peaks are the *overlaps*: `wmap` + all 12 `sub` maps coexist (:998 clears wmap only after the full partition copy); `mts` + `out` coexist (:1082-1084); XICs + `flat` coexist (:2424 builds the grid, :2432 frees the XICs after). The peak driver is the **widest window's trace-phase working set** — it holds ~23.7% of precursors (:2244), so scale everything ~5x: ~2 GB compact → ~25-40 GB transient, overlapping 7 average windows × ~9 GB + 55 GB base ≈ your 151.7 GB peak. Note the peak is *worse* than your 97 GB delta suggests: the compact store is consumed as windows materialise (:491-495), so the base is shrinking while windows grow — contemporaneous in-flight footprint is ~110-115 GB, ~14 GB/window. Also `win_out` accumulates all ~863k spectra (~5-7 GB) until the loop ends (:2256, merged :2639).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:32:  **The 10x + 256 MB estimate (:2318-2321) is not defensible as calibrated.** It books the average window at 8.9 GB against a measured ~12-14 GB — under by ~35-55% in the unsafe direction (it admits more than will fit), while over-booking the wide window by luck. It was fitted on S23 (:2315-2317), and the multiplier is threshold-dependent (`trace:ms2_noise_threshold_int` 10 inflates trace count → FragGrid). The fixed `inflight` charge is also held for the window's whole lifetime while real footprint swings ~3x between trace and score phases.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:38:  MS1_TRACE: invert Amdahl on your numbers — 83 = S + (410.9 − S)/12 gives **S ≈ 53 s of strictly serial work, 64% of the phase**. It is all in `detectTraces_`'s serial prologue/epilogue: the quantile sampling and full partition copy of every MS1 peak into 12 sub-PeakMaps (:955-997, single thread, ~2-3×10⁸ peaks), the merge (:1016-1018), the span instrumentation (:1065-1081), and `toTrace` over 6.4M traces (:1082-1085, per-trace sort). More bands cannot help — the parallel fraction is already small. Parallelising the partition (order-preserving chunked append per band) and `toTrace` (independent per trace, index-stable) is output-neutral and worth ~45-50 s wall (~3.5%). Worth fixing *after* the big items; it is low risk, not high value.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:46:  1. **It traces summed frames; you trace raw ones.** diaTracer sums adjacent cycles into a composite (m/z × 1/K0) matrix *before* peak picking (documented in your own `aggregateFrames_` header, :290-303). Point volume into its trace-equivalent stage drops ~Nx and S/N rises ~√N, so far fewer noise fragments survive. You centroid every raw frame and trace at noise 10 (:786, `ms2_min_length_sec` 0), producing up to 8.5M fragment traces in one window. Trace (48.7%) and score (46.1%) both scale with trace count — score superlinearly via candidate pairs.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:47:  2. **Your m/z tracer is superlinear in local density; theirs is a matrix extraction.** Your own sweep proves it: banding cuts *total* trace CPU 11,168 → 5,378 → 2,972 s across 5/12/24 bands (:2379-2383). A linear algorithm shows flat CPU under partitioning; halving the CPU by halving the band means MassTraceDetection's per-apex candidate scan is doing quadratic-ish redundant work. diaTracer never pays it.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:48:  3. **Correlation candidate volume.** You linearly scan the whole IM band per precursor (:1500-1502) and RT-gate *after* (:1505); with ~932k hypotheses (≈40% guessed singletons, :2082-2083) × an IM band holding 5-15% of 8.5M fragments, the scan itself is ~10¹⁰-10¹¹ element visits per window before a single dot product — the dot only runs for the ~0.3% passing the 3 s RT gate. diaTracer correlates per detected feature against intensity-ranked fragment sets with a pre-smoothed precursor profile: fewer, cheaper pairs.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:49:  4. **Duplicate edge work**: 12 bands × 20 ppm halo (:975) + the 1.000 Da window overlap → every boundary ion traced and scored twice (~14% combined).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:57:  **R1 — Parameter-only grid rebalance: `-perf:trace_bands 24` (→ n_conc = 4 × 24 = 96) and `16` (→ 6 × 16).** The S30 wall loss at 24 bands was *oversubscription* (24 concurrent windows × 24 bands, :2383-2385) — the current code divides the budget (:2297), so 4 × 24 = 96 is not oversubscribed, and trace CPU halves per your own measurement. Score's `p_team` becomes 25, halving per-window score wall. Naive model: window-time sum 8,139.5 → ~3,300-4,500 s, wall ~830-1,000 s (**−10 to −25%**). Memory peak drops ~2x (4 windows in flight). Risks: 4 slots × 136x skew could resurrect the tail (heavy-first :2253 mitigates); the trace-CPU-halving may not transfer from S30. Output-neutral (band partition is exact, :946-951). Falsify: two runs, read the `[perf]` table. Costs nothing but machine time — run it today.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:59:  **R2 — 2D (IM, RT) candidate index in `scoreCandidates_` (:1500-1505).** The linear IM-band scan is plausibly 80-90% of score cost (RT gate kills ~99.7% of scanned elements before the dot at :1521). Bucket the IM-sorted fragments into ~256 IM buckets, RT-sort within each, enumerate by binary search, then sort surviving `fi` ascending to preserve emission order (the tie-break at :1664-1665 uses vector index — order must be identical). Expected: score window-time 3,753.6 → ~500-900 s; **−20-27% total wall, −30% CPU**, and a smaller working set (helps if R1's perf-stat shows bandwidth stalls). Output-neutral by construction. Risk: medium — new per-window index (~1-2 s build), and a wrong index silently changes the candidate set. Falsify cheaply *before writing it*: add two atomic counters (IM-band elements scanned vs dot products evaluated) and run once — if scan/dot < 5x, deprioritise; then verify with a byte-diff of the canonically sorted (:2649) mzML on S30.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:61:  **R3 — Parallelise `buildFragGrid` (:714-744) and skip the `wmap` middleman.** fraggrid is 395.8 s of single-thread work holding an 11-thread team hostage; the per-fragment indexing is independent and the grid build can be 12-way merged deterministically. Materialising compact frames *directly into band maps* (edges from the same every-97th-peak sample, :956-957, computable on the compact store) deletes the `wmap` + `sub[]` coexistence — the biggest transient copy (~2x compact per window). Gain: ~4-5% wall, plus several GB/window of peak RSS, which also makes the admission model honest. Output-neutral; falsify by mzML diff + stage table. Medium risk (edge-identical partition logic duplicated).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:65:  **R5 — Make admission self-calibrating.** Replace the fixed 10x (:2318-2321) with a multiplier re-fit from measured per-window RSS deltas (you already read `/proc` per phase, :106-111), and log per-window admit-wait to settle whether R1's 10.7% is tail or memory. No wall gain on this node; prevents OOM-serialization on shared ones. Low risk, output-neutral.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:67:  **R6 (last, and say it plainly) — the output-changing one.** `trace:native_ms2_neighbors` (:830) is diaTracer's pre-centroid frame summing, the actual 2-3x CPU lever; the tool-level version already lost 26% of peptides (:830 comment), so this needs the full benchmark gate, not a performance review's blessing. Combined ceiling for R0-R5: wall ~750-900 s, CPU ~45-55k s — still ~1.5x diaTracer. **Closing the remaining gap is an algorithm change, not an optimisation, and it will not pass a peptide-set-equality gate for free.** Anyone who tells you otherwise is selling a rewrite.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:5:- Acceptance is not simply “within `mass_error_ppm`.” OpenMS initializes a standard deviation from the ppm value, tests candidates within **±3 standard deviations**, and dynamically re-estimates that deviation by default. See [`MassTraceDetection::findBestPeak_` and `isPeakAcceptable_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:288), [`MassTraceDetection::processPeak_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:384), and the default `reestimate_mt_sd=true` in [`MassTraceDetection::MassTraceDetection`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:26).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:6:- `min_sample_rate` does not terminate extension in this configuration. speXtract changes its value but leaves `trace_termination_criterion` at OpenMS’s default `"outlier"`; the sample rate is therefore applied as a final validity filter. See [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:979), [`MassTraceDetection::MassTraceDetection`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:40), and [`MassTraceDetection::isTraceValid_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:445). The comments at [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:987) are inaccurate on that point.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:10:1. **Halo sizing: correct.** The asymmetric arithmetic was a real defect; the current working-tree fix sizes each side from its own edge in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1044). But `20.0` remains an unsupported empirical constant. Nothing in the detector establishes a 20-ppm-multiple span bound.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:12:2. **Trace drift: correct in conclusion and stronger than stated.** The running intensity-weighted centroid is updated after each accepted peak, while the allowed standard deviation can also expand. See [`MassTraceDetection::updateIterativeWeightedMean_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:62), [`MassTraceDetection::processPeak_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:417), and [`MassTraceDetection::run_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:537). Even with a fixed tolerance, repeated movement relative to a running mean has no input-independent total-span bound. With variance re-estimation, the failure can be much faster. speXtract also leaves maximum trace length disabled unless explicitly positive in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:991).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:14:3. **Detection non-locality: correct, but the argument is incomplete.** OpenMS processes intensity-ordered apices through one greedy `peak_visited` state; only peaks belonging to valid traces become visited. Changing the peak set can therefore change later seeds and assignments. See [`MassTraceDetection::run`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:216) and [`MassTraceDetection::run_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:471).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:16:There is an additional, cleaner non-locality defect: **speXtract drops a spectrum completely from a band if it has no raw peak inside that band’s halo** in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1041). OpenMS distinguishes an absent spectrum from a present spectrum containing an out-of-tolerance peak when counting misses and visited scans. Thus even a zero-width mass trace can change validity under banding.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:18:Valley splitting is not an independent source of the initial discrepancy. It runs only after band results have been filtered and concatenated in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1090) and [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1100). It can amplify an already altered trace, but if the incoming `MassTrace` set and memberships were identical, per-trace splitting would not manufacture a band-count dependency.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:20:The centroid-core filter is also weaker than its comment claims. It suppresses duplicate detections only when independent band runs reconstruct the same trace with the same centroid. It cannot prevent two changed reconstructions from landing in different cores, or both reconstructions from moving away from the original core. See [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1073). Ownership moreover uses the pre-split intensity-weighted `MassTrace` centroid, whereas emitted `Trace::mz` may be the apex, median, or mean in [`toTrace`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:275).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:26:Use 21 consecutive spectra, constant IM, `mass_error_ppm=15`, `min_trace_length=0`, valley splitting off. The first peak has intensity 100; all later peaks have intensity 99. Starting at 1000, choose each next m/z to be the current running centroid plus `2.9 × current_sd`, so it is inside OpenMS’s `3 × sd` acceptance window:
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:53:Because every spectrum has one peak, the two-band quantile edge is sample element 10: `1001.069497614`, under the calculation in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1026).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:62:The unbanded detector accepts all 21 points and produces a true intensity-weighted centroid of approximately `1001.784379570`, in the upper core. Neither band contains all 21 points. The lower band receives points 0–11 and can produce a different trace with centroid approximately `1000.460927687`; the upper band lacks points 0–8 and cannot reproduce the unbanded trace. No subsequent canonical sort can repair different memberships; the sort only normalizes ordering in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::main_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2538).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:84:In the full map, the 1000 trace has three hits over seven adjusted scans: `3/7 < 0.5`, so [`MassTraceDetection::isTraceValid_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:445) rejects it. In the lower-band map, the four internal 2000 spectra do not exist because [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1068) only appends nonempty band spectra. The three 1000 spectra become adjacent in the sub-map, yielding quality `3/3`; the trailing 1500 misses are terminal and are subtracted by the OpenMS validity calculation. The identical zero-span trace is rejected unbanded and retained banded.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:88:Finally, “no halo width can repair this” is literally too strong. A halo covering the entire window makes every band run on the whole map and is exact after core ownership—but it performs essentially N full detections. What does not exist is a useful finite halo derived solely from `mass_error_ppm`.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:94:| **(a) Halo from a real trace-span bound** | **Not from span alone.** Exact only if the bound covers the detector’s entire causal influence and reproduces full-map scan/miss semantics. | Running centroid and adaptive SD make the worst-case algorithmic bound approach the entire m/z range. A whole-window halo is exact but duplicates essentially all peaks and all MTD work N times. | Compare exact per-trace peak memberships and validity with `bands=1`, not just centroids. Include drift, competing-apex, missing-spectrum and threshold-boundary synthetic tests. |
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:95:| **(b) Partition at large m/z gaps** | **Not as stated.** A gap wider than every final trace is insufficient because unrelated peaks across the gap still determine whether a spectrum is “nonempty” and therefore whether a miss is counted. | It can be exact only with a proven separation of the complete interaction graph and faithful preservation of scan-presence semantics. Components may be extremely uneven or collapse into one component, defeating load balancing. Current sampled quantiles do not search for gaps; they deliberately target load balance in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1024). | For each proposed component, compare the detector’s peak-assignment partition and rejected traces against a full-window run. Test spectra populated exclusively by peaks in other components. |
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:96:| **(c) Detect the whole window** | **Yes.** This is the reference semantics. | It gives up the reduction in candidate population and within-window trace parallelism: approximately the stated 18 minutes/160 GB instead of 12:58/102 GB. It can still run different windows concurrently subject to memory admission. | Repeat `bands=1` for determinism, then make it the oracle for every optimization. Require exact trace membership and final semantic equality. |
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:97:| **(d) Declare banding approximate and pin it** | **No, but reproducible.** | Fix N and the edge algorithm independently of thread count. Different worker counts may schedule the same fixed tasks, but cannot change the scientific partition. “Independent of band count” is possible only by removing band count as a variable or making the algorithm genuinely exact. | Verify identical output across thread counts at fixed N, then publish N-versus-1 error measurements. |
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:99:The only straightforward exact implementations are whole-window detection or the degenerate whole-window halo. A useful gap partition might be exact, but the proof obligation is substantially stronger than “no final trace crosses the gap.”
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:112:**Thread-derived auto should not exist for scientific output.** The current MS2 auto mode reads `omp_get_max_threads()` and computes `ceil(threads/windows)` in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::main_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2366). That makes the input, parameters, and software insufficient to specify the result: the available machine changes N, and N changes the accepted-peptide set.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:141:At the trace stage, dump or hash exact trace membership before valley splitting: window, input frame, peak m/z, IM and intensity for every member. After optimal matching by member overlap, report:
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:163:**Pipeline parallelism: yes. Equivalent within-window MTD parallelism: no, not under the “do not rewrite OpenMS” constraint.**
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:165:The current master/worker pool already provides exact parallelism across isolation windows in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::main_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2440). Valley splitting and default precursor scoring also create independent tasks in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1115) and [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::main_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2610). With `bands=1`, different windows can therefore be tracing, splitting and scoring concurrently.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:167:But the task pool does not make one MTD call parallel. OpenMS’s trace detector still walks the intensity-ordered apex list serially through one shared visited set in [`MassTraceDetection::run_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS-mzpeak-build/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.cpp:471). During a phase when every in-flight window is inside MTD, useful trace concurrency is capped by the number of admitted windows—at most the acquisition’s window count and possibly less under the memory gate.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:169:Therefore the statement that bands are “no longer needed to fill cores” is only partly true. They are no longer needed to avoid the old rigid nested-team grid, but the band `taskloop` still creates the additional MTD work units in [`TOPPDIAPseudoSpectra::detectTraces_`](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1073). Removing it also removes within-window trace concurrency.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:173:- One unbanded MTD task per window, with current heaviest-first and memory-bounded scheduling.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:174:- Overlap whole-window MTD tasks with EPD and scoring tasks from other windows.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:176:- Reduce concurrent windows if the 160-GB path threatens memory; this sacrifices wall time, not correctness.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.md:178:The measured superlinear benefit is algorithmic, not merely parallel: smaller bands reduce each MTD call’s candidate population. Consequently, there is no wrapper-level trick that simultaneously preserves whole-window semantics, per-band peak counts, current memory, and current speed. One of those properties must be surrendered.
