docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:231:This confirms the mechanism. Key facts from OpenMS MassTraceDetection:
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:234:  - Acceptance window: `centroid_mz ± 3*ftl_sd`, where `ftl_sd = (centroid_mz/1e6) * mass_error_ppm_` initially (line 537) — so the per-peak acceptance window is ±3 ppm (3 sigma), and the centroid moves with each accepted peak. So per-step tolerance is 3x mass_error_ppm, and it's against the running centroid. Note: `reestimate_mt_sd_` — when true, ftl_sd is dynamically re-estimated via updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust. What's the default? Let me check defaults_ in MassTraceDetection.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:236:  Also findBestPeak_ picks the peak closest to the centroid within the ±3sd window (for IM data: closest in m/z among IM-matching peaks). So if two peaks in the same spectrum both within window, the nearest to centroid is picked — dependent on centroid, dependent on peak set.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:238:  Important: since acceptance window is ±3*ftl_sd where ftl_sd = centroid*ppm/1e6, the per-step tolerance is 3x mass_error_ppm. So a halo of 20x ppm covers ~6.6 step widths. But drift is unbounded in principle: centroid is intensity-weighted mean of all peaks; each new peak can be up to 3sd from the current mean, and the weighted mean moves toward it by w*intensity/sum. Actually with intensity weighting the mean can move by at most (3sd * I_new / (I_sum + I_new)). If new peaks have high intensity relative to accumulated, mean moves fast. So over a long trace the centroid can walk — each step bounded by 3sd*I_new/Itot, cumulative over N peaks could be up to ~3sd * sum of weights — data-dependent, unbounded a priori. Confirm user's hypothesis 2.
