docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:269:  - `reestimate_mt_sd` default "true" (line 34) — ftl_sd dynamically re-estimated via `updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust`, so the acceptance window is ±3σ of the observed within-trace m/z variance, which itself depends on the peak set. And the speXtract wrapper doesn't set reestimate_mt_sd, so it inherits "true". This is important: the acceptance window isn't even fixed at ±3*ppm — it can shrink/grow with the observed variance. So per-peak tolerance isn't even bounded by 3×mass_error_ppm after re-estimation. Actually is there a cap? updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust computes a robust SD estimate. Let me check whether it's capped. In ElutionPeakDetection/MassTrace code... `updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust` is in OpenMS FILTERING/DATAREDUCTION? Actually in OpenMS source, updateWeightedSDEstimateRobust is defined in MassTraceDetection.h or elsewhere. Let me check quickly.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:271:  Also important: findBestPeak_ picks the peak with the CLOSEST m/z to centroid among IM-eligible peaks in window — so if a halo excludes a peak that would have been the closest match, a different peak is picked (mechanism 3). Also peak_visited bitset: peaks consumed by earlier (higher-intensity-apex) traces are unavailable — greedy competition across the whole map; banding changes which traces compete with which.
