docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:406:The robust SD update: sd_t grows when |p.mz - mean| is large relative to current sd, and decays ~1/sqrt(weights_sum). There's no cap tying it back to mass_error_ppm. So with reestimate_mt_sd=true (default, not overridden in DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp — I should verify it isn't set anywhere), ftl_sd can exceed ppm-based value, and the acceptance window is centroid ± 3*ftl_sd with a moving centroid. Actually note sd can also decay: denom1 = log(weights)+2log(sd) contributes sd²*weights_sum_old; tmp_sd = sqrt( (w_old·sd²) + I·d² ) / sqrt(w_old + I) roughly — it's a running weighted RMS. So if peaks track tightly, sd shrinks and the window tightens; if they scatter, window widens. So the per-step tolerance is itself data-dependent. This kills any fixed halo bound.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/kimi.err:410:  Wait — one subtlety: the docs in speXtract say min_sample_rate "terminates trace extension when occupancy drops" — that's only true in "sample_rate" termination mode. In default "outlier" mode, extension stops after 5 consecutive misses, and min_sample_rate is only a post-hoc validity filter (isTraceValid_). Let me check lines 589/642 — those are the sample_rate checks, guarded by termination mode. Let me verify:            if (trace_termination_criterion_ == OUTLIER)
