docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:178:  Question 1 binding constraint answer: The binding constraint is the correlation kernel's raw work volume + trace stage raw work (they're 95% of window-time), NOT the concurrency cap: the grid is 96/100 threads and even a perfect scheduler only recovers the 29% idle → best-case wall ~1139×0.71≈810 s for the loop; total ~1,020 s vs diaTracer 586 s. So scheduling alone cannot close the 2.3× wall gap; work reduction must come from the algorithm side. Which numbers would have to be wrong: the stage sums (8,139.5) and window-loop CPU (77,333). If the 67.9x already reflected max achievable... The claim "29% recoverable by scheduling" fails if: (a) the admission/tail idle (972 outer-s) is actually forced by memory (admission serialized because budget < need — then it's a memory constraint, not scheduling; settle by logging admit-wait vs window count; if waits dominate, raising perf:mem_fraction or reducing per-window footprint is the fix, not scheduling); (b) if the 12× reservation model overstates (e.g., memory-bandwidth saturation: 96 threads streaming FragGrid at ~35 GB/window could be bandwidth-bound, making "idle" threads actually stalled on memory — settle with perf stat: measure memory bandwidth / stalled cycles during score stage; if bandwidth-saturated, the score index fix helps doubly by shrinking the working set).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:180:  Also self-contradiction check on user numbers: "mean cores busy 60 of 128" overall vs LOAD 81.7x + window 67.9x weighted: (55.2×81.7 + 83×5 + 50.6×1 + 1139×67.9 + 2×1 + 21.1×1)/1351 = (4510+415+50.6+77339+2+21.1)/1351 = 82,338/1351 = 60.9. ✓ consistent with 60. And 22.9 h CPU = 82,440 s ✓ ≈ 82,338. Consistent.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:186:  One more subtle contradiction to probe: window-loop CPU 77,333 vs stage-sum×12 model: stage wall sums 8,139.5; if trace ran 12-thread and score 12-thread fully, CPU would be ~12×(3,965.6+3,753.6)+1×(24.1+395.8+1.0) ≈ 93,051 — exceeds 77,333 → inner fill ≤ 0.815 as computed. No contradiction, just fill.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:188:  Another check: is the claim "executed at most 8 windows at a time" consistent with 8,139.5/1,139 = 7.15 avg windows in flight — yes ≤ 8.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:190:  Also the 1.000 Da window overlap: fragments within 1 Da of both edges are traced and scored TWICE (in two windows) — by design (each window independent). Cost ≈ overlap fraction of m/z range... small (~1-2%). Not the issue.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:192:  Now memory estimate per window, more carefully:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:193:  - compact avg 0.87 GB/window.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:195:  - Trace phase peak per window ≈ wmap (1.74) + sub (1.9) + MTD internals. MTD holds mass traces: 8.5M × 168 B header = 1.4 GB + trace peaks (Peak2D 32 B × total support). If total support ≈ 4.3e9 for big window... no wait, that was grid points? The FragGrid comment: "8.5 M fragments x ~510 support points is within 2x of the 32-bit wrap" — 8.5e6×510 = 4.3e9 entries in flat. So the BIG window has ~4.3e9 support points → flat = 34.7 GB (8 B each: int+float). That alone ~35 GB for one window. With need = 10×compact+0.256: big window compact ~5 GB (23.7% of 20.8) → need ≈ 50 GB. So admission grants 50 GB — plausible. But average need ≈ 8.9 GB. Hmm, but then 8 × 12 GB measured = 97 GB — the average includes phases. FragGrid big-window 34.7 GB >> 12 GB avg. So peak RSS 151.7 = base 55 + mix. If two big windows' FragGrids coincide... only one window is 141 Da wide. Fine — numbers roughly cohere: 55 + 8×9 ≈ 127... measured 151.7. The extra ~25 GB ≈ one big-window FragGrid/MTD transient. OK.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:197:  Peak-driving allocation: candidates: (a) FragGrid.flat for the widest window (~35 GB), (b) MTD trace vectors + EPD copies for big window, (c) sub[] band copies. The instrumented answer: log RSS per stage per window — the code already logs rss at phase ends but not per-window-stage. The [mem] logs at :2402 (window done) print RSS per window completion — could extract from logs. Peak driver most likely the widest window's trace→grid overlap: sub copies (7-9 GB) + MTD traces + Trace XICs + FragGrid construction: buildFragGrid copies xic into flat while xic still live: 4.3e9×8 + XIC 4.3e9×16 = 34.7 + 69 = 104 GB for the widest window?! That can't be — peak total is 151.7 with 55 base and other windows. Hmm, so 8.5M×510 must not be the widest window on THIS file, or support is smaller. The comment hedges "up to" / "within 2x of wrap". If actual support avg ~50: flat = 8.5e6×50×8 = 3.4 GB, XIC = 6.8 GB. Fine. I should be careful: cite the comment as evidence the design anticipates up to ~510, and note the discrepancy is measurable. The 12 GB/window decomposes roughly: wmap 2× + sub 2× + traces/XIC ~4-6× + grid ~4× compact — sum of phase peaks ~10-14× compact ≈ matches 12 GB vs 0.87 compact ≈ 14×. So 10×+256MB admission UNDER-estimates by ~1.4× on average and more on big windows → admission control is over-optimistic: it admits 8 windows believing 8×8.9=71 GB while they actually consume 97 GB. On this node it survived; on a smaller node it OOMs. Defensible? Direction is right (proportional to compact), multiplier measured on S23 (~5.8 GB vs 0.56 compact ≈ 10.4×) but S23 windows may be smaller/lower-threshold; the multiplier is threshold-dependent (ms2_noise 10 → more traces → FragGrid bigger). Make the multiplier adaptive: measure actual RSS delta per completed window and re-fit. Or set 14×. Low-risk fix.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:203:  Bigger memory→speed lever: fewer windows in flight (4) with 24 bands halves loop memory (97→~50 GB) — same experiment as the grid A/B.
