docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:262:  Now Q1 "which of my numbers would have to be wrong for your explanation to fail": My decomposition rests on: stage wall sums (8,139.5) ≈ outer-busy time; CPU 77,333; wall 1,139. If stage sums were actually CPU-s (mislabeled) or if admit-waits are large (memory-bound rather than scheduling), the conclusion "inner imbalance 14.4% + tail/admission 10.7% + serial 4.2%" shifts. Specifically: if admit-wait ≈ 0 (measurable), then the 972 s outer gap is pure tail/heavy-first granularity → window rebalance won't help, only smaller work units. If per-band timings show balance, then inner idle is memory-bandwidth stalls → the score index/footprint fixes matter more than scheduling. Ask for: per-window admit-wait, per-band wall, per-stage CPU.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:265:  1. **Input-volume to the tracer**: diaTracer sums adjacent cycles pre-centroid (S/N ↑ ~√N, point count ↓ ~N×) so its peak-picking/trace stage sees ~1/5 the points and far fewer noise traces. speXtract traces every raw frame at noise 10 → 2.19e9 peaks compacted, 8.5M traces in big windows. Trace+score are ~95% of window CPU; both scale (super)linearly with trace count. This alone can be ~2-3×.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:266:  2. **Superlinear m/z tracer**: MTD's per-apex candidate scan makes tracing superlinear in local peak density (proven by own measurement: banding cuts TOTAL CPU 11,168→2,972 s from 5→24 bands — a linear algorithm would show flat CPU). diaTracer's matrix-extraction approach doesn't pay this.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:267:  3. **Correlation candidate volume**: every precursor × every fragment in the IM band, linear-scanned then support-walked; ~1M precursor hypotheses incl. ~40% guessed singletons + fan-out 6.45 share-all. diaTracer correlates per detected precursor FEATURE with intensity-ranked fragment sets and retains top-500 by intensity — fewer, cheaper pairs. Also RT gating by apex distance only after full scan.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:268:  4. **Redundant halo work**: 12 bands × 20 ppm halo + 1 Da window overlap → duplicated tracing at every edge (~12% + ~2%).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:269:  5. Valley-splitting EPD over 8.5M traces/window (+38% wall documented) — diaTracer doesn't split OpenMS-style traces because it doesn't build them.
