docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:303:  Also: memory peak driver statement: the peak-driving allocation is most plausibly the widest window's (FragGrid.flat + still-live Trace XICs + sub[] band copies + MTD trace store) transient — during buildFragGrid, xic (16 B/pt) and flat (8 B/pt) coexist: 24 B/pt; if the wide window has ~4e8-4e9 support points → 10-90 GB. Given measured +97 GB total, and the compact store for the wide window ~5 GB, a 24 B/pt structure with 5 GB×(1 pt per ~10 B... compact peaks ≈ support points lower bound... support points ≤ peaks. Wide window peaks ≈ 5e8 (5 GB compact at 10 B) → XIC 8 GB + flat 4 GB + wmap 10 GB + sub 10 GB... these don't all coexist: wmap freed at :998 (before MTD completes? map.clear(true) at :998 happens right after partition, BEFORE band tracing — so during band tracing: sub[] (12 maps, ~10 GB incl halo) + growing MTD traces. After: traces+XICs (~16+ GB), then +flat (~8 GB) while xic alive. Peak per wide window ~25-35 GB. So peak total 151.7 = 55 base + ~7 avg windows × 9-10 + wide-window transient ~30 ≈ 150. ✓ Consistent. So the peak driver = widest window's trace-phase working set (sub copies + MTD traces + XIC + grid). Good.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:305:  Is 10× + 256 MB defensible? For the wide window: need = 50 GB vs actual transient ~30 GB → over-covered; for average window: need 8.9 vs actual ~10-12 → under-covered. It's a crude linear model; direction fine, calibration ±40%. The bigger issue: `inflight` is charged `need` for the WHOLE window lifetime, but actual footprint swings ~4× between phases; and admission re-reads MemAvailable which includes page cache — on a 128-core shared node with big page cache, MemAvailable is optimistic. It's defensible as a heuristic but not as a guarantee; the measured 97 GB delta vs 8×8.9=71 GB booked proves the booking under-covers by ~35% in practice. Recommend self-calibrating multiplier (measure RSS delta per window, adapt) — cheap, keeps shared-node safety.
