docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:365:  - window overlap 1 Da — from user.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:376:  One more check on buildFragGrid cost driver: the union-grid build pushes EVERY xic point into g.rt then sorts (F×S log) — 8.5M×50 = 4e8 doubles sort per big window, single thread: ~10-20 s. Plus per-point lower_bound log(G). fraggrid 395.8 s total window-wall across 24 windows ≈ 16.5 s avg — consistent with serial sort+index. Parallelise: per-thread partial vectors + k-way merge, or construct grid from frame RTs directly! Actually the grid is the union of fragment RTs — but every fragment XIC point comes from a frame RT of that window; the union of xic RTs ⊆ window frame RTs. g.rt could just be the sorted unique frame RTs of the window (known before tracing, from CompactFrame.rt!) — no giant sort needed; deterministic; and lower_bound per point still needed but could use frame index directly... The grid = union of fragment RTs — equal to frame RTs if every frame contributed ≥1 fragment, else a subset. Using ALL frame RTs as grid would add empty grid points → changes G → changes Pearson (variance over full grid G!) → OUTPUT CHANGE. So must keep union-of-fragment-RTs semantics. Can still build faster: collect per-fragment sorted xic RTs (each sorted), k-way merge via... or parallel partition by RT range: each thread builds sorted sub-vector for its fragment chunk, then merge 12 sorted vectors (parallel pairwise merge). Deterministic. Fine — details for the implementation; keep the recommendation.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:378:  Also for the score index: note frag_im sorted (:2417-2418, fragments sorted by im then mz then rt). The scan lo..hi over IM band then RT-gates. Build: bucket the IM-sorted array into blocks (e.g., 1024 fragments), keep per-block min/max RT → skip blocks whose RT range doesn't overlap [pc.rt-3, pc.rt+3]? RT within block unsorted (sorted by mz within same im? sort key is (im,mz,rt,intensity) — within an IM band block, RT effectively unsorted). Block min/max precompute: cheap, exact (no false negatives), skips blocks fully outside RT window. With 3 s window over ~30 min gradient, ~99.7% blocks skipped → scan cost ~300×... but blocks spanning 1024 fragments across diverse RTs will have wide min/max... fragments within a tight IM slice come from all RTs — block min/max RT ≈ full range → no skipping. Better: within coarse IM buckets (say 256 buckets over the window's IM range), sort fragments by RT → two binary searches per bucket. Enumerate candidates bucket-by-bucket; to keep global fi-ascending order for tie-break safety, collect candidate fis then sort them (candidates per precursor small after RT gate, ~thousands) — sort ascending fi → identical emission order. Index build: F log F per window (~8.5M×log ≈ 200M ops ≈ 0.2 s×12 threads parallelisable, or just do it single-threaded ~1-2 s vs 156 s avg score window-wall). Fine.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:380:  Expected gain quantification for R1: score window-wall 3,753.6 s. Scan share est: scan visits per precursor ≈ |IM band|. Assume scan ≈ 80-90% of score CPU (RT gate kills ~99%+ before dot). New score ≈ 0.1-0.2× old + index build. Score wall-sum → ~400-800 s. Window-loop wall → from 1,139 to ~750-800 s (sum/8: (24.1+3965.6+395.8+600+1)/8 ≈ 623 + fill losses... hmm sum becomes ~4,987/8 = 623 s + same idle structure ≈ 700-790 s). Total wall → ~970-1,060. That's -22-28%. And CPU 77.3k → ~47-52k (-35%). Then mean cores busy drops; diaTracer gap narrows to ~1.8× CPU.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:382:  Careful not to over-promise: present as estimated range, falsify with one instrumented run (log scan-visits vs dot-evaluations counters — 2 atomic counters, trivial).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:384:  Actually a cheaper falsification FIRST: add counters (candidates scanned vs dots computed) — one run tells you scan share before writing the index. Good "falsify cheaply".
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:390:  Let me also answer "which of my numbers would have to be wrong": The 29%-idle decomposition fails if (i) stage wall sums are wrong (timers measure something else — but they're chrono deltas around each stage per window, code :2355-2434,2517-2534 — sound); (ii) the 77,333 CPU includes massive spin (then inner "idle" isn't recoverable); (iii) admission waits ≈ 972 outer-s are memory-forced not scheduling (then raising concurrency OOMs). Each has a cheap measurement. Also if "60 of 128 mean" is right (it checks out arithmetically), the ceiling claim stands: even perfect packing gives loop wall ≈ CPU/96 = 806 s → total ~1,020 s ≈ still 1.74× diaTracer wall. So scheduling alone can't reach parity — work must shrink. That requires only CPU=77,333 and threads=96 to be right.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:396:  Also mention: the 4 idle threads (100-96) — could set n_bands... 100/12=8.33→8; could use n_conc=8, bands=12, +LOAD uses 100. Or 9 windows × 11 bands = 99? n_conc=min(24, 100/11=9), bands 11 → 99 threads. Marginal (+3%). Not worth vs R2.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:400:  Base 55 GB: compact 20.9 + ms1_map during MS1 trace... at loop start 55 GB includes compact 20.9 GB; the rest ~34 GB = ms1_traces (6.4M × xic ~ 16 B×~20 pts + 64 ≈ 2.5 GB) + precursors + PeakMap overhead of... hmm 34 GB unexplained. MS1 picked map: MS1 = 1,343 frames × ~690k RAW peaks; after PeakPickerIM centroiding much less, say 100-200k/frame → 1.5-2.7e8 peaks × 20 B ≈ 3-5.4 GB + FloatDataArray... Let me not over-derive; base is measured 55 GB and mostly freed/consumed through loop (compact consumed per window). Actually — the compact store is consumed as windows materialise (:491-495), so base SHRINKS during loop while windows add. Peak 151.7 with base shrinking means in-flight peak is even higher than 97 GB relative to contemporaneous base. Note this: late in the loop, compact store is mostly freed (say half → -10 GB), so in-flight windows at peak moment ≈ 97 + up to ~10-20 GB of still-unconsumed compact ≈ 110-115 GB → ~14 GB/window. Slightly strengthens "admission under-covers".
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:404:  End RSS 87,886 MB after loop: compact freed, windows freed, but all_out 7 GB + ms1_traces + base stuff. 87.9 GB residual at SORT — that includes... whatever; peak was 151.7 mid-loop.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:406:  OK. Also one more structural observation for Q1: `schedule(dynamic)` + admission inside iteration + heavy-first: the 8 threads grab the 8 biggest windows first; these also have the largest `need`. If budget only fits, say, 6 of them, two threads SLEEP while... they can't take smaller windows (dynamic assignment already made at iteration start? With schedule(dynamic), chunks assigned on demand — thread requests next iteration when free. The assignment order is wi order. A thread stuck in admit loop for window 9 holds its assignment; another finishing thread takes window 10 (smaller, may fit). So smaller windows CAN pass a blocked big one — good, no head-of-line blocking beyond one slot. OK fine.)
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:410:  Let me also double-check user's stage table claim "executed at most 8 windows at a time" — but the sum 8,139.5 includes score executed with 12 threads per window; window-wall per window is the window's own elapsed; 8 concurrent → sum/8 ≤ wall ✓ 1,017 ≤ 1,139.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:416:  Q2 numbers: per-window footprint table (avg window compact 0.87 GB):
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:422:  - So a big window's trace-phase peak ≈ wmap 1.7 + sub 1.9 + traces ~2-4 + XIC 3-7 ≈ 9-14 GB; score-phase ≈ XIC freed but grid 3.5 + scalars 1 + pdense ≈ 5-6 GB. Average over phases × 8 ≈ 12 GB/window-time ✓ explicable WITHOUT invoking leaks. But: transient coexistence (wmap+sub; xic+flat; mts+out) means ~30-40% of the peak is copies that need not coexist. And the widest window (compact ~3-5 GB) scales all of this ~4-6× → 40-60 GB transient — that's the peak driver.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:424:  The 10×+256 MB estimate: prices the AVERAGE window at ~8.9 GB vs actual ~12 → under by ~35%; for the widest window need ~30-50 GB vs transient ~40-60 → roughly right by luck. As a safety mechanism on a shared node it's miscalibrated in the unsafe direction for average windows (books less than it uses → admits more than fit). Recommend measuring actual.
