docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:16:The mechanism is the m/z BANDING of mass-trace detection, and the question is whether it is exact.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:20:`detectTraces_()` is given a window's peak map and a band count N (`perf:trace_bands`, default 12):
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:25:   `[edge[b] - h_lo, edge[b+1] + h_hi)`, where the HALO h is 20x the `trace:mass_error_ppm`
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:28:3. Each sub-map is run through `MassTraceDetection` independently and concurrently.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:33:The parameter's help text claims: "Traces cannot span more than trace:mass_error_ppm in m/z, so
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:42:`MassTraceDetection` in OpenMS grows a trace by accepting the next peak within `mass_error_ppm` of
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:43:the trace's RUNNING centroid, and it also applies `min_sample_rate` (terminating extension when
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:56:   differ between band counts, because MassTraceDetection's result depends on the SET of peaks it
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:59:   `min_sample_rate` and valley splitting compound this: occupancy and valley shape are computed
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:73:   than any possible trace span, so bands never cut a trace; (c) detecting on the whole window and
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:84:   window loop's time and the tool now runs a master/worker OpenMP task pool over all threads, so
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:86:   MassTraceDetection's cost is superlinear in peaks (4 bands measured 18:25 wall against 12:58 at
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:97:/bin/bash -lc "pwd && rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'SKILL.md' -g 'src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp' -g '*MassTraceDetection*' -g '*ElutionPeakDetection*'" in /Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:166:/bin/bash -lc 'wc -l src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp && rg -n "detectTraces_|trace_bands|MassTraceDetection|ElutionPeakDetection|mass_error_ppm|min_sample_rate|halo|band|semantic|digest|accepted" src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp' in /Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:169:18:#include <OpenMS/FEATUREFINDER/MassTraceDetection.h>
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:171:57:  IM-aware (@ref MassTraceDetection with an ion-mobility tolerance), then fragments are
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:172:250:  /// MassTraceDetection locates the per-peak IM array by the exact name
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:174:499:  /// Rebuild a PeakMap for ONE window from its compact frames, for MassTraceDetection.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:175:509:      s.setMSLevel(1); // MassTraceDetection traces MS1-level only
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:176:605:      // MS1 stays a PeakMap (MassTraceDetection needs it whole), but its pick is NOT small: 1,343
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:177:848:    registerDoubleOption_("assembly:im_weight_sigma", "<1/K0>", 0.0, "[recall] Down-weight each emitted fragment's intensity by a Gaussian of its ion-mobility distance from the precursor, exp(-(dIM)^2 / 2 sigma^2). TIMS precedes fragmentation so a TRUE fragment shares the precursor's 1/K0 exactly; a co-isolating interferent at the edge of the gate:delta_im band is suppressed. WITHIN-spectrum (no fragment removed or redistributed across precursors) so it escapes the fan-out depletion that falsified competitive/apex/deconvolution, and directly un-buries faint true fragments under the engine's intensity top-150. 0 = off. Try ~0.005 (half the 0.01 IM gate). [bench]", false);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:178:854:    registerTOPPSubsection_("trace", "Mass-trace detection (see MassTraceDetection)");
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:179:855:    registerDoubleOption_("trace:mass_error_ppm", "<ppm>", 15.0, "m/z tolerance for trace detection.", false);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:180:871:    registerDoubleOption_("trace:ms2_split_valleys", "<chrom_fwhm>", 7.0, "[way-2] Split MS2 mass traces at chromatographic local minima via ElutionPeakDetection (which MassTraceDetection never does - it only terminates on outliers, so two peptides 25 s apart within the m/z tolerance MERGE). Value is chrom_fwhm in seconds; it sets both the Savitzky-Golay window and the local-extrema half-window. 0 = OFF. Try 6-8 (peaks here are 5-30 s). width_filtering is forced off so this only SPLITS, never deletes.", false);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:181:875:    registerDoubleOption_("trace:ms1_split_valleys", "<chrom_fwhm>", 7.0, "Split MS1 mass traces at chromatographic valleys via ElutionPeakDetection. DEFAULT 7.0 s: MassTraceDetection never splits at a local minimum, so two peptides eluting ~25 s apart within the m/z tolerance merge into ONE multi-modal trace, producing a merged precursor with the wrong monoisotope and charge. Measured +36.7% peptides on S23 and +37.4% on S08 at peptide-level FDR. Costs ~1.7x wall time and ~25% peak RAM. 0 = off.", false);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:182:876:    registerDoubleOption_("trace:max_trace_length_sec", "<sec>", -1.0, "DANGER - NOT a guard. MassTraceDetection::isTraceValid_ RETURNS FALSE on over-length traces and peaks are marked visited only for VALID traces, so an over-length blob is rejected, re-seeded by the next apex, and rejected again - emitting NOTHING at that m/z. Setting this DELETES signal (the falsified direction). Kept only for diagnostics; leave at -1. Splitting requires ElutionPeakDetection, not this. [merged-trace]", false, true);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:183:879:    registerDoubleOption_("trace:ms2_min_sample_rate", "<r>", -1.0, "MS2 min fraction of visited scans that must contain a peak (MassTraceDetection min_sample_rate). OpenMS default 0.5 hard-deletes gappy fragment traces; MEASURED: 0.3 and 0.1 both LOSE peptides (8,865 -> 8,735 at 0.3), so -1 (= OpenMS 0.5) is the default. Lowering it produces longer, more-merged traces with wrong apexes.", false);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:184:880:    registerDoubleOption_("trace:ms1_min_sample_rate", "<r>", -1.0, "MS1 min_sample_rate. -1 = OpenMS default 0.5 (strict, MS1 stays strict).", false, true);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:185:908:    registerIntOption_("perf:ms1_trace_bands", "<n>", 12, "[parallel] Band-parallel MS1 mass-trace detection, same exact-partition scheme as perf:trace_bands. MS1 tracing was running SINGLE-THREADED (the call simply never passed a band count) while MS2 was banded, so the MS1 phase pinned one core. That is invisible at default thresholds because MS1 is only ~4% of frames, but it makes the MS1 SENSITIVITY sweep impossible: lowering trace:noise_threshold_int / ms1_chrom_peak_snr explodes MassTraceDetection's intensity-sorted apex list, and MassTraceDetection is a SERIAL greedy loop, so runtime blows up superlinearly on one core (measured: 40 min with no progress at 1.5 of 128 cores, vs 24 min for a whole default run). 1 = off (old behaviour).", false);
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:186:909:    registerIntOption_("perf:trace_bands", "<n>", 12, "[parallel] Split each window's fragment m/z range into N bands traced CONCURRENTLY. The diaPASEF method fixes the window count (24 here), which caps window-level parallelism far below the core count; banding lifts the ceiling to threads. Traces cannot span more than trace:mass_error_ppm in m/z, so banding with a halo is exact. 0 = auto (windows x bands >= threads), 1 = off.", false);
