docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2252:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_review_codex2.out:2105:- **Peak RSS may increase substantially during Phase A.** `MassTraceDetection::run()` copies qualifying spectra and peaks into its local `work_exp`. With several windows processed concurrently, memory includes all original window maps, multiple active `work_exp` copies, `MassTrace` results, and retained `WindowFrags`. Clearing each source map only *after* detection cannot reduce the transient detection peak. Given the existing 72 GB RSS, this needs measurement and probably concurrency throttling; otherwise the parallel version may simply run out of memory.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2253:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_review_codex2.out:2172:- **Peak RSS may increase substantially during Phase A.** `MassTraceDetection::run()` copies qualifying spectra and peaks into its local `work_exp`. With several windows processed concurrently, memory includes all original window maps, multiple active `work_exp` copies, `MassTrace` results, and retained `WindowFrags`. Clearing each source map only *after* detection cannot reduce the transient detection peak. Given the existing 72 GB RSS, this needs measurement and probably concurrency throttling; otherwise the parallel version may simply run out of memory.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2254:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_code_review_kimi.out:317:  But wait - we should double-check that `detectTraces_` doesn't store references. Let's look at `MassTraceDetection::run`. It takes `const PeakMap&` and produces `vector<MassTrace>`. MassTrace objects in OpenMS contain a vector of `MassTracePeak` (or `Peak2D` copies). Each MassTracePeak stores the actual data. So no references. The `toTrace` function copies everything into `Trace`. So safe.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2255:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_code_review_kimi.out:327:  21. **`MassTraceDetection::setLogType(ProgressLogger::NONE)`**: Called inside `detectTraces_`, which is serial. Good. If it were called in parallel, the `setLogType` on a shared object could race. But it's serial.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2256:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/xic-construction.md:31:- `min_sample_rate`.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2257:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/xic-construction.md:64:- **`min_sample_rate` warning shot.** That change (+9 frags, −130 peptides) shows admitting marginally-supported chromatographic objects is expensive. `width_filtering=fixed` + `masstrace_snr_filtering=false` admits exactly that class (short sub-peaks carved from a noisy parent).
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2258:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/RANKED-SUGGESTIONS.md:64:`min_sample_rate` result (+9 frags, −130 peptides) has another explanation.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2259:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/RANKED-SUGGESTIONS.md:99:`masstrace_snr_filtering=false` in arm B1 and **`=true` in arm B2** (the `min_sample_rate` result is a
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2260:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/RANKED-SUGGESTIONS.md:148:- `min_sample_rate` relaxation (+9 frags, −130 peptides)
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2261:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/ion-mobility-xic.md:39:`min_sample_rate` 0.5 chains any point within ppm tolerance, so two co-eluting species at the same m/z (routine in a wide diaPASEF isolation window) fuse into one elongated trace with a single wrong apex. Two measurements point here: (i) relaxing `min_sample_rate` 0.5→0.1 gained 9 fragments but **lost 130 peptides** — the signature of longer, more-merged traces with wrong apexes/shapes; (ii) our Pearson has degenerated into a co-location measure, which a merged trace corrupts maximally, since it co-locates with everything under it. Splitting restores per-species apexes, making the +/-3 s apex gate and any shape score meaningful, and improves precision without cutting fragment counts — the one direction the falsification table leaves open. See [[zero-padded-pearson-defect]].
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2262:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/ion-mobility-xic.md:42:73.6% of missing peptides are absent at *any* score; that absent set is 65% charge 2+. Splitting merged MS2 traces cannot make an absent precursor appear — it only re-attributes fragments among precursors already detected. Only MS1 splitting adds precursors, and MS1 is where merging is rarest (same m/z within ppm **and** same 1/K0 within 0.01 **and** overlapping RT). Running the min_sample_rate axis backwards should return the same order of magnitude (~130). This is a precision patch, **not** a closer for the 3,857 gap.
docs/reviews/banding-halo-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:2263:/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/ion-mobility-xic.md:53:- KILL if **<10% of MS2 traces split** (elongation premise false; the min_sample_rate result has another explanation).
