docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7784:- **BLOCKER — malformed or unsupported MS2 input can yield a valid-looking empty/partial result.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1787`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1787>), [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1838`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1838>), [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2850`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2850>): validation checks only that MS1 has some IM data. MS2 frames without IM or precursors are silently skipped, zero windows are allowed, and zero emitted spectra are written with `EXECUTION_OK`. Smallest fix: count and reject missing-IM/missing-window MS2 frames, require at least one usable isolation window, and fail if all windows or all output spectra disappear.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7786:- **BLOCKER — the default contiguous isotope walk is a known wrong-mass generator.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1163`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1163>), `inferPrecursors_()`: the first missing isotope terminates each direction. Higher isotopes are then left unmarked and can seed new precursors with an M+1/M+2 monoisotope. The project’s own baseline confirms this mechanism at `S30-BASELINE.md:703-713`. Smallest fix: separate scoring from ownership—scan the bounded isotope lattice for the selected charge despite gaps, then mark every matched member of the selected envelope as consumed. Rebenchmark before adoption.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7790:- **HIGH — window identity is lossy.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1723>) and [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:602`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:602>): windows are keyed only by m/z bounds rounded to 0.01 Da; mobility bands and `swath_nr` are discarded. Distinct windows can be merged, particularly methods reusing an m/z interval at different mobility bands. Smallest fix: use a structured acquisition-window identity containing unrounded/canonically quantized m/z and IM bounds, and abort on inconsistent collisions.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7792:- **HIGH — precursors on a shared window boundary are emitted twice.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2394`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2394>): `lower_bound(win_lo)` plus `upper_bound(win_hi)` makes both edges inclusive. Adjacent windows `[a,b]` and `[b,c]` both claim a precursor exactly at `b`. Smallest fix: use half-open intervals `[lo,hi)`, with an explicit exception only for the final acquisition boundary.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7798:- **HIGH — IM units are assumed, not enforced.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1796`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1796>): mzML is accepted after detecting “some IM,” but milliseconds, CCS, and FAIMS values can then be treated numerically as 1/K0. That silently corrupts every IM gate. Smallest fix: verify VSSC/1/K0 units on every relevant spectrum and reject unsupported units with a conversion-oriented error.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7804:- **MEDIUM — CSR indexing narrows real input sizes to 32 bits.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:273`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:273>) and [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:725`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:725>): `FragGrid::off` is `uint32_t`, and grid positions are `int`. A sufficiently large window wraps offsets and silently reads the wrong fragment support. Given this tool already operates near hundred-gigabyte scale, this is not academic. Smallest fix: use `size_t` for offsets and indices, or abort before exceeding the chosen representation.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7808:- **MEDIUM — even aggregation sizes do not mean what the parameter says.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:300`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:300>): `n_frames=4` gives `half=2`, hence a five-frame window. Smallest fix: reject even values or implement asymmetric bounds totaling exactly `n_frames`.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7812:I found no confirmed C++ data race in the reviewed OpenMP window loop or MS1 pick loop: picker instances are `firstprivate`, spectra/slots are disjoint, `pdense` is copied per inner worker, and shared appends/errors are protected. I also found no unchecked `--iterator` decrement; the only `prev(it)` at line 1442 is guarded. Empty `lower_bound`/`upper_bound` calls themselves are safe. The remaining risks are deterministic algorithmic wrongness and incomplete total ordering, not an obvious unprotected write.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7818:- **HIGH — the documented “current defaults” are false.** [`S30-BASELINE.md:20`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/S30-BASELINE.md:20>) says `ms2_noise_threshold_int=10` and `ms2_split_valleys=7`; code defaults are 100 and 0 at [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:780`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:780>) and line 788. Thus the README’s headline numbers are not reproducible from its one-line default invocation. Smallest fix: either change the defaults or label the benchmark overrides explicitly in README and provide the exact INI.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7822:- **HIGH — `trace:split_valleys_fwhm` describes the wrong code path.** [`DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:791`](</Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:791>) says it overrides `trace:ms1_split_valleys`; lines 1969–1976 modify only `ms2_split`. Smallest fix: change the help to “overrides `trace:ms2_split_valleys`,” or implement the claimed MS1 two-pass behavior.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7836:The registered falsified options do exist with defensible off defaults: `competitive` false, `apportion=0`, `rp_max=0`, frame aggregation `=1`, `variance_support` false, `consolidate:delta_rt=0`, `merge:rt_window=0`, `mono_averagine_guard=0`, and `mono_averagine_select` false. The exception is `ambiguity_margin`: it exists and defaults to zero, but its advertised count-mode experiment never exercised the implementation.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7848:- **HIGH — no realistic resource requirements are given.** The README recommends tcmalloc but the benchmark itself reports 100–156 GB peak RAM. The admission code deliberately admits one oversized window, so a normal workstation can still OOM. Smallest fix: document tested RAM/disk/core requirements, a low-memory command, expected slowdown, and the meaning/limits of `perf:mem_fraction`.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7852:- **MEDIUM — the input contract is incomplete.** README does not specify that mzML must contain usable MS1 and MS2, per-peak VSSC IM arrays, isolation-window offsets, and suitable profile/centroid metadata. It also does not describe behavior for unsupported IM units or multiple precursor records. Smallest fix: add an “Input contract” table and a preflight command.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7907:4. **Window-partition test.** Two adjacent windows `[400,425)` and `[425,450]`, with precursors just below, exactly on, and just above 425. Assert each is assigned exactly once. Add two equal m/z windows with different IM bands to catch key collisions.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7911:6. **Band-equivalence test for `detectTraces_()`.** Construct a trace drifting across a quantile boundary and compare `bands=1` versus `bands=12` trace sets bit-for-bit. Include a valley-split trace and duplicate boundary m/z.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7917:9. **Invalid-input/parameter tests.** Missing MS2 IM, no isolation windows, zero usable fragments, negative counts/tolerances/memory fraction, invalid environment integers, and an unreadable `SPEXTRACT_PRECURSOR_LIST` must all fail loudly rather than write an empty mzML.
docs/reviews/release-2026-09-03/codex.transcript:7921:Current verification is not sufficient for a public release. Peptide-set equality is downstream and lossy: it can miss dropped frames, duplicated spectra, wrong charges, isotope-shifted monoisotopes, tiny intensity corruption, and empty experimental branches whenever the search still returns the same peptides. Closed-search equality is especially incapable of protecting the blind/open-search failure modes that define this tool. The existing calibration tests cover the calibration formula, not the loader contract, precursor inference, window partitioning, OpenMP equivalence, or spectrum assembly.
