 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Window-loop audit — where the 3,805 s goes
     2	
     3	Scope: `src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1526-1720` (the `#pragma omp parallel for` over the 24 diaPASEF
     4	isolation tiles) and everything it calls, including OpenMS
     5	(`/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS/src/openms/`).
     6	
     7	Every line reference below was checked against the working tree at `787441a`
     8	(`src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp`, 1,932 lines) and against the OpenMS source on this machine. Claims
     9	are labelled **MEASURED** (a number that exists in a log) or **INFERRED** (derived from code
    10	structure plus arithmetic). The distinction is the point of this document: almost everything
    11	previously asserted about the inside of the window loop is inferred, and one widely repeated
    12	"measurement" is not a measurement at all.
    13	
    14	Quality constraint: the S30 baseline is **10,072 peptides** (`docs/S30-BASELINE.md:11`), noise
    15	floor 0.06% same-node, "differences below ~0.2% mean nothing". A speedup that costs peptides is
    16	not a speedup.
    17	
    18	---
    19	
    20	## 1. WHERE THE LOOP'S TIME GOES
    21	
    22	### 1.1 MEASURED
    23	
    24	S30 (9.8 GB, smallest sample), 120 threads, tcmalloc already `LD_PRELOAD`ed:
    25	
    26	| phase | time | share | source |
    27	|---|---:|---:|---|
    28	| load + pick + split | 32 s | 0.8% | `clk_()` at `:1257`, `:1314` |
    29	| MS1 tracing + inference | 330 s | 7.9% | `clk_()` at `:1314` → `:1458` |
    30	| **window loop** | **3,805 s** | **91.3%** | `clk_()` at `:1458` → `:1736` |
    31	| total | 4,167 s | | |
    32	
    33	| resource | value |
    34	|---|---:|
    35	| wall | 4,428 s (01:13:48) |
    36	| CPU total | 64,521 s (17:55:21) |
    37	| user | 36,456 s (10:07:36) |
    38	| **system** | **28,065 s (07:47:45) = 43.5% of CPU** |
    39	| effective cores (CPU/wall) | **14.57** of 120 |
    40	
    41	diaTracer does the same job in 457 s → we are **9.1×** slower.
    42	
    43	### 1.2 The window loop is ONE undivided number — this is the central measurement gap
    44	
    45	`phase_clock_()` is defined at `:77`, wrapped by `clk_()` at `:82`, and called at exactly five
    46	sites: `:1222`, `:1257`, `:1314`, `:1458`, `:1736`. **There is no timer anywhere between `:1526`
    47	and `:1720`.** "WINDOW LOOP 3,805 s" is therefore a single opaque interval covering nine distinct
    48	stages. Every attribution below it is inferred.
    49	
    50	### 1.3 The loop's structure — MEASURED from code, not timed
    51	
    52	- `n_threads = omp_get_max_threads()` = 120 (`:1483`; `TOPPBase.cpp:86` applies `-threads 120`,
    53	  `harness/bench.py:110`).
    54	- `n_bands = ceil(120 / 24) = 5` (`:1489-1492`), `perf:trace_bands` default 0 → auto.
    55	- `n_conc = min(24, 120/5) = 24` (`:1497`) — i.e. **every window is admitted at once**.
    56	- `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) num_threads(n_conc)` over 24 iterations with 24
    57	  threads: **`schedule(dynamic)` is inert.** One window per thread, no work stealing, no
    58	  rebalancing. The loop's wall time is the *slowest single window*, not the mean.
    59	- Admission (`:1535-1546`) is a `for(;;)` poll with `sleep_for(200 ms)` inside
    60	  `#pragma omp critical(admit)`, re-reading `/proc/meminfo` each pass. 24 × ~5.8 GB ≈ 139 GB
    61	  against the ~150 GB observed resident ⇒ all 24 were admitted; throttling is not the limiter.
    62	
    63	### 1.4 What runs at what width inside one window — INFERRED from code
    64	
    65	| lines | stage | threads |
    66	|---|---|---|
    67	| `:1549-1551` | `materializeWindow` + `sortSpectra` (~56 M peaks, ~1.1 GB) | 1 |
    68	| `:633-649` | band partition, `bands` full passes over every peak, both output vectors unreserved | 1 |
    69	| `:652-666` | `mtd.run` per band (`num_threads(bands)`) | **5** |
    70	| `:684-718` | ElutionPeakDetection valley split, 32 batches of 250 k | **nested, see §2** |
    71	| `:721-737` | `[merged-trace]` RT-span instrumentation, 64 MB alloc + 8 M-element sort | 1 |
    72	| `:738-741` | `toTrace` × ~8 M (each with its own `sort`) | 1 |
    73	| `:1568-1573` | canonical sort of 8 M × 56 B = 448 MB | 1 |
    74	| `:1579` | `buildFragGrid` (sorts ~56 M doubles ≈ 450 MB to recover ~1,343 uniques) | 1 |
    75	| `:1591-1712` | scoring, ~40 k precursors | 1 |
    76	
    77	### 1.5 Two arithmetic facts that constrain any attribution
    78	
    79	**(a) Band parallelism contributes almost nothing to the time integral.** If `mtd.run` at `:652`
    80	dominated the loop, the run would show ≈ 24 × 5 = 120 effective cores. It shows **14.57**. Even
    81	crediting *all* measured CPU to the loop gives 64,521 / 3,805 = **17.0 concurrent** — below 24,
    82	i.e. below one busy thread per window. The wall sits in code running at ~one thread per window.
    83	
    84	**(b) Scoring cannot be the bottleneck.** `scoreCandidates_` is one call per precursor on the
    85	default path (`:1648` → `:1168`); the apportion (`:1616`/`:1627`) and `rp_max` (`:1663`) paths are
    86	default-off (`:496-497`) and both are on the falsified list (`docs/S30-BASELINE.md:35-36`). At
    87	~2.2 ms/precursor × ~40 k precursors that is **~90-170 s of a ~3,805 s per-window wall = 2-4%**.
    88	Independently: the claimed ~22 TB of gate-scan traffic over 3,805 s is 5.8 GB/s aggregate on a
    89	node that sustains ≥100 GB/s, capping the whole scan at ~12% even in the worst case.
    90	
    91	**Conclusion.** The time is in the single-threaded stages of the window body and in the one stage
    92	that is neither single-threaded nor sanely parallel — the nested EPD region. §2 argues the latter.
    93	
    94	### 1.6 The instrumentation that must exist before any further ranking
    95	
    96	Add `phase_clock_()` deltas around `:1549`, `:1555` (split into MTD / EPD), `:1568`, `:1579`,
    97	`:1591`, plus per-window wall, reduced in the `#pragma omp critical` that already exists at
    98	`:1557`. Six calls. Until they exist, everything in §1.4 is a hypothesis with a plausible
    99	mechanism, not a budget.
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	## 2. THE 43.5% KERNEL TIME
   104	
   105	### 2.1 The single best-supported explanation: a 24×-oversubscribed nested OpenMP region
   106	
   107	Six facts, each verified at a cited line, compose into one mechanism:
   108	
   109	1. **MS2 valley splitting is ON.** `trace:ms2_split_valleys` registers a default of `0.0`
   110	   (`:514`), but `bench/run_open.sh:42` and `docs/S30-BASELINE.md:21-22` pass **7.0**. So
   111	   `:684` (`split_valleys > 0.0 && !mts.empty()`) is true on every window and
   112	   `ElutionPeakDetection::detectPeaks` runs *inside* the window body.
   113	
   114	2. **Nested parallelism is enabled.** `:1502` executes `omp_set_max_active_levels(2)` whenever
   115	   `n_bands > 1`, which is the default configuration. Level-2 regions are therefore **active**,
   116	   not serialized.
   117	
   118	3. **The EPD call site is at nesting level 2.** The band region closes at `:666`;
   119	   `epd.detectPeaks(chunk, part)` is at `:708`, inside the window loop but outside the band loop.
   120	
   121	4. **EPD's parallel region has no thread cap.**
   122	   `/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS/src/openms/source/FEATUREFINDER/ElutionPeakDetection.cpp:326-329`:
   123	   ```cpp
   124	   #ifdef _OPENMP
   125	   #pragma omp parallel for
   126	   #endif
   127	       for (SignedSize i = 0; i < (SignedSize) mt_vec.size(); ++i)
   128	   ```
   129	   No `num_threads` clause ⇒ team size = `nthreads-var` = **120**.
   130	
   131	5. **⇒ up to 24 × 120 = 2,880 live OS threads on 120 cores.** Entered 32× per window
   132	   (`BATCH = 250000` at `:696` over ~8 M traces) = **768 fork/join cycles of a 120-thread team**,
   133	   each with a full barrier, while 23 other windows are doing the same thing.
   134	
   135	6. **Every result leaves through a program-global lock.**
   136	   `ElutionPeakDetection.cpp:459-463` and `:544-548` are
   137	   `#pragma omp critical (OPENMS_ElutionPeakDetection_mtraces)` — a *named* critical, which in
   138	   OpenMP is one lock **per program**, not per region. Roughly 8 M emits/window × 24 ≈ **192 M
   139	   contended acquisitions**, each doing a heap-allocating deep copy (`push_back` of a `MassTrace`
   140	   that has no move constructor) plus periodic vector reallocation **inside the lock**.
   141	
   142	**24× oversubscription is the term that generates system time.** 2,880 runnable threads on 120
   143	cores means every OpenMP barrier and every `critical` exhausts its spin budget before the holder
   144	is even scheduled, and falls through to `futex(FUTEX_WAIT)`/`sched_yield`. That is charged as
   145	kernel time and shows up as involuntary context switches, which is exactly the observed 43.5%
   146	and the 14.57/120 efficiency.
   147	
   148	### 2.2 Two aggravating factors at the same call site
   149	
   150	- **`ElutionPeakDetection.cpp:334-336`**: `#pragma omp atomic ++progress` executes **per trace**,
   151	  outside the `IF_MASTERTHREAD` guard — one contended RMW on a single shared cache line by a
   152	  120-thread team, ~8 M times per window.
   153	- **`ElutionPeakDetection`'s constructor calls `setLogType(CMD)`
   154	  (`ElutionPeakDetection.cpp:43`)** and `DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:686` never silences it — unlike
   155	  `mtd.setLogType(ProgressLogger::NONE)` at `:584` ("quiet + thread-safe (called from the parallel
   156	  window loop)") and `m2.setLogType` at `:655`. `startProgress`/`endProgress` mutate
   157	  `ProgressLogger::recursion_depth_`, a **non-atomic `static int`**
   158	  (`ProgressLogger.cpp:125`, `++` at `:238`, `--` at `:268`), from 24 concurrent window threads.
   159	  That is a genuine data race, independent of how much time the logger costs.
   160	
   161	### 2.3 What this explanation does NOT claim
   162	
   163	**The named critical alone does not account for 43.5%.** Measured aggregate lock throughput with
   164	a `MassTrace`-sized payload is ~16.6 M emits/s at 16 threads. 192 M emits ⇒ ~12 s of fully
   165	serialized wall and ~170-550 CPU-s of contention — **0.3-0.9% of 64,521 s, not 43.5%**. The lock
   166	is the *funnel*; the 2,880 threads queuing at it are the cost.
   167	
   168	Page faults do not account for it either. The three largest fault sources (band partition at
   169	`:633-649` ≈ 550 k/window, `materializeWindow` ≈ 280 k, `buildFragGrid` ≈ 165-230 k) total under
   170	1 M minor faults per window ≈ 24 M for the run ≈ **~24 s** at 1 µs each — three orders of magnitude
   171	short of 6.3 cores permanently in the kernel. THP on `madvise` with no `madvise` call anywhere in
   172	the tool is a real observation and worth a `MADV_HUGEPAGE` experiment, but it is a hypothesis, not
   173	the explanation.
   174	
   175	### 2.4 The falsifying measurements — run these first
   176	
   177	1. **`grep Threads /proc/$(pgrep -f DIAPseudoSpectra)/status` during the window loop.**
   178	   ~120 ⇒ this whole section is wrong. ~2,900 ⇒ confirmed. One command, decides everything.
   179	   Note the log line at `:1504-1507` is **not** evidence either way: it prints
   180	   `String(n_conc * n_bands) + " live threads"`, the tool's own arithmetic over the band loop,
   181	   which does not know EPD exists.
   182	2. **`OMP_NUM_THREADS=120,1`** — the correct zero-code A/B. The list form sets `nthreads-var`
   183	   per level; both explicit `num_threads` clauses (`n_conc` at `:1526`, `bands` at `:652`)
   184	   override it, so *only* EPD's bare `parallel for` is capped.
   185	   **`OMP_MAX_ACTIVE_LEVELS=1` does nothing** — `:1502` overrides the environment ICV at runtime.
   186	   Verified: `OMP_MAX_ACTIVE_LEVELS=1` leaves the EPD team at 16/16 and total live threads
   187	   unchanged; `OMP_NUM_THREADS=16,1` drops it to 1.
   188	3. `perf stat -e context-switches,minor-faults` and `perf top -g -K` / `-U` to split kernel time
   189	   between futex and fault handling.
   190	
   191	---
   192	
   193	## 3. OUTPUT-IDENTICAL FIXES, ranked by (expected speedup)/(risk)
   194	
   195	"Output-identical" here means: the emitted mzML is byte-identical, **or** identical to within the
   196	run-to-run nondeterminism the current code already has. That caveat is necessary and is stated per
   197	fix: the canonical comparator at `:1568-1573` sorts on `(im, mz, rt, intensity)` with no index
   198	tie-break, `std::sort` is not stable, and today's trace insertion order is already
   199	thread-schedule-dependent through the EPD critical — so the 10,072-peptide baseline is **not
   200	bit-reproducible today**.
   201	
   202	### OI-1 — Cap the nested EPD team. `src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:686-716`. **← IMPLEMENTED**
   203	
   204	**Change.** Immediately before the batch loop that calls `epd.detectPeaks`, and only when already
   205	inside a parallel region, set the *calling task's* `nthreads-var` to the same inner budget the
   206	band loop was given (`bands`); restore it after. `omp_set_num_threads` writes only the calling
   207	task's ICV, so it cannot disturb the explicit `num_threads(bands)` clause at `:652`, and the
   208	`omp_get_level() > 0` guard leaves the **MS1** call (`:1345`, level 0, `ms1_split_valleys` default
   209	7.0) at the full 120 threads where that parallelism is legitimate. Also silence EPD's
   210	`ProgressLogger`, matching `mtd` two lines up.
   211	
   212	**Expected gain.** Live threads during the EPD stage drop from ≤ 2,880 to `n_conc × bands` ≈ 120,
   213	i.e. exactly the thread budget. If §2 is right this removes the bulk of the 28,065 s of system
   214	time. A conservative reading — kernel time falling from 43.5% to the ~10% a well-behaved OpenMP
   215	program shows — implies the loop's 3,805 s falls to roughly 1,600-2,400 s, a **1.6-2.4×** on the
   216	loop and ~1.5-2.1× overall. If §2 is wrong the change is a no-op, not a regression.
   217	
   218	**Why it cannot change output.** It changes only how many threads execute a loop whose iterations
   219	are independent (`detectElutionPeaks_(mt_vec[i], single_mtraces)`, each reading a distinct
   220	`mt_vec[i]`). The *set* of emitted sub-traces is thread-count-invariant. The *order* in which they
   221	land in `single_mtraces` is today decided by which thread wins the critical — i.e. already
   222	nondeterministic — and becomes closer to deterministic index order under the cap. Downstream, the
   223	MS2 traces are canonically re-sorted at `:1568-1573` before anything reads them. Net:
   224	**set-identical, and strictly more deterministic than today.**
   225	
   226	**Falsifying measurement.**
   227	- `grep Threads /proc/<pid>/status` mid-loop still shows ~2,900 ⇒ the cap did not take (libgomp
   228	  and libomp both honour the per-task ICV, but verify).
   229	- Window-loop wall does not move and kernel share stays ≥ 40% ⇒ §2's mechanism is wrong; the time
   230	  is elsewhere and the phase timers of §1.6 become mandatory.
   231	- S30 peptides move by more than the 0.06% noise floor ⇒ the order argument above is wrong and the
   232	  change must be reverted.
   233	
   234	### OI-2 — Give `MassTrace` a move constructor. `OpenMS/.../KERNEL/MassTrace.h:66-72`
   235	
   236	**Change.** Two lines: `MassTrace(MassTrace&&) = default;` and
   237	`MassTrace& operator=(MassTrace&&) = default;`. `MassTrace.h:66,69,72` user-declare the destructor,
   238	copy constructor and copy assignment (`= default` still counts as user-declared), which by
   239	`[class.copy.ctor]/8` **suppresses the implicit move members**. `MSSpectrum.h:173/182` and
   240	`MSExperiment.h:107/113` do declare theirs — `MassTrace` is the outlier, so this is an OpenMS
   241	defect, not a design choice.
   242	
   243	**Expected gain.** Measured: `std::move(mt)` into a `vector<MassTrace>` is currently a deep copy
   244	(`src.getSize()` stays 7 after the move); `push_back` costs 5.277 allocations/trace unreserved,
   245	2.000 reserved. With the patch, 0.001/trace. Every `std::move` in `detectTraces_` (`:664`, `:669`,
   246	`:703`, `:710`) plus `MassTraceDetection.cpp:675` and `ElutionPeakDetection.cpp:463/548` — the last
   247	two **inside** the global critical — become real moves. ~6-10 deep copies of every trace per
   248	window eliminated, and the lock hold time in OI-1's funnel becomes constant instead of
   249	occasionally O(n).
   250	
   251	It also repairs three things silently:
   252	- `:706` `mts[i] = MassTrace()` currently **frees nothing** (measured over 20,000 traces:
   253	  `new=0 free=0`) because the rvalue binds to `const MassTrace&` and `vector::operator=` retains
   254	  capacity. With the patch: `new=0 free=40000`. The documented memory optimisation starts working
   255	  *as written*, with trace order preserved — which is why this is the right fix and reverse-iterate
   256	  + `pop_back` is not.
   257	- `:699` `out.reserve(n_in)` is a **guaranteed** under-reserve (the adjacent comment says "split
   258	  only ever grows the count"), so `out` reallocates and deep-copies millions of traces.
   259	- `:708`'s `part` is unreserved and filled *inside* the program-global critical.
   260	
   261	**Why it cannot change output.** A defaulted move and a copy produce identical values.
   262	`OpenMS::String` is nothrow-move-constructible so the defaulted move ctor is `noexcept`, meaning
   263	`vector` growth uses it. No numeric path changes. **Bit-identical by construction** — this is the
   264	only fix on the list with no ordering exposure at all.
   265	
   266	**Falsifying measurement.** Rebuild libOpenMS; any change in S30 peptide count at all (not just
   267	beyond noise) falsifies the "identical values" claim. Memory: per-window peak RSS should drop
   268	visibly from ~5.8 GB.
   269	
   270	**Blocker.** Requires rebuilding `libOpenMS`, not just the TOPP tool, so it is out of scope for
   271	the single-file build path used here.
   272	
   273	### OI-3 — Gate the `[merged-trace]` instrumentation. `src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:721-737`
   274	
   275	**Change.** The block is guarded by `if (!mts.empty())` — **only the string assembly at `:734` is
   276	guarded by `span_log`**. So a full scan of every peak of every one of ~8 M traces, a 64 MB
   277	`vector<double>`, and an 8 M-element `sort` run **unconditionally, per window, serial**. The result
   278	is printed only when `win_done == 0` (`:1559`). **23 of 24 windows compute it and throw it away.**
   279	Add a `bool want_spans` parameter and gate the whole block.
   280	
   281	**Expected gain.** ~0.6 s/window of pure diagnostics on the critical path, plus 64 MB of dead
   282	allocation and ~8 M page touches per window. Small in wall terms, free in risk terms.
   283	
   284	**Why it cannot change output.** `spans` is never read by anything but the log string. Note a
   285	latent bug it also fixes: `:714` appends the valley-split summary to `*span_log` and `:734`
   286	**assigns** over it, so the valley-split line is currently discarded whenever splitting ran.
   287	Gating restores it — a *log* change, not an mzML change.
   288	
   289	**Falsifying measurement.** Any diff in the emitted mzML. There should be none.
   290	
   291	### OI-4 — SoA the RT gate. `src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1074-1075`, build at `:1574`
   292	
   293	**Change.** `:1074-1075` binds `const Trace& f` and reads **only `f.rt`** — 8 bytes at offset 8 of
   294	a 56-byte object — to reject ~99.7% of the IM band (`gate:delta_rt` 3.0 at `:488`, against a
   295	1,857 s gradient). 24 of those 56 bytes are the `xic` vector header, permanently
   296	`{nullptr,nullptr,nullptr}` after the explicit swap at `:1585`. Build a `vector<double> frag_rt`
   297	alongside the existing `frag_im` at `:1574-1575` and gate on that.
   298	
   299	**Expected gain.** 7.0× less traffic on the gate scan: ~15.7 MB → ~2.24 MB per precursor
   300	(`N_band ≈ 8 M × 0.035 ≈ 280 k`, using the project's own measured ±0.01 1/K0 slab occupancy at
   301	`docs/REVIEW-2026-07-22.md:672-674`). This is ~3-5% of the loop *and* it relieves a shared
   302	resource: 24 concurrent streams at 7 GB/s each is 168 GB/s of demand on a node that sustains
   303	~120 GB/s, so the per-thread rate is below 7 GB/s today and cutting traffic helps every thread.
   304	
   305	**Why it cannot change output.** Same `double`, same `fabs`, same comparison, same iteration
   306	order — a pure reordering of the same memory. **Placement is load-bearing**: `frag_rt` must be
   307	built after the `remove_if` at `:1563` *and* after the sort at `:1568`, because `fi` indexes the
   308	post-sort array. That is exactly where `frag_im` is already built, so the pattern is proven
   309	in-tree.
   310	
   311	**Cost the fix owes.** +64 MB/window × 24 = **1.5 GB RSS** on a memory-bounded run. The strictly
   312	better version packs the four scalars into a 32-byte SoA and drops `frag_traces` entirely after
   313	`:1585`, which *saves* 192 MB/window.
   314	
   315	**Falsifying measurement.** mzML diff (should be empty); loop wall should move by ≤ 5%, so if it
   316	moves by 30% the model of where time goes is wrong.
   317	
   318	### OI-5 — Release `g.rt`'s capacity. `src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:440-442`
   319	
   320	**Change.** `g.rt` accumulates **every** XIC RT of every fragment — ~56 M doubles, unreserved
