docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:9:The window loop alone consumes 77,333 CPU-s = 21.48 CPU-h—2.94× diaTracer’s entire 7.3 CPU-h. Even deleting every other speXtract phase would not produce CPU parity. Scheduler improvements can plausibly remove 15–25% wall time. Closing the remaining gap requires reducing the number of fragment traces and/or precursor–fragment correlation work.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:17:The per-window stage timers are elapsed stage durations summed across windows—not CPU-seconds, despite the incorrect log message at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2671](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2671). The timers surround stages with `steady_clock`, for example [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2415](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2415) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2472](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2472).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:23:- Average cores used per active window:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:33:| Known serial stages | at least 4.06 | Materialize + old serial `fraggrid` + emit = 420.9 of 8139.5 window-s, using one rather than 12 cores |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:40:- **Eight-window cap:** not the primary limit. Eight windows × twelve threads already exposes 96 threads. The four permanently unused threads cost at most 4.2%. The rigid topology is bad chiefly because idle teams cannot help surviving windows, which the current task-pool rewrite explicitly addresses at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2363).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:42:- **Across-window imbalance/admission:** measurable but bounded. Perfectly filling eight slots reduces the loop from 1139 to \(8139.5/8 = 1017.4\) s: at most 121.6 s, or 9.0% of total wall time. Because admission waiting is outside the stage timers, the supplied data cannot separate window-size tail from memory admission.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:49:- **Raw trace/correlation volume:** the actual binding problem. Trace and score occupy 94.83% of summed active window time. The scoring path takes an IM slice, performs the RT gate, then walks every surviving fragment’s CSR support for the dot product at [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1515) and [DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1534](/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:1534). With up to 8.5 M fragment traces per window, this is an enormous intermediate workload unrelated to the final count of 863 k spectra.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:56:- Exactly eight window workers and real 12-thread nested teams were used.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:58:- Admission did not regularly restrict execution below eight windows.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:59:- The timers measure elapsed per-window stage duration—as the source shows—rather than CPU time as the log incorrectly claims.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:61:The settling measurement is cheap: record, per window and band, admission-wait time, stage start/end, thread CPU time and active-team size. One OMPT trace or even four atomic histograms would separate admission, window tail, band imbalance and memory stalls.
