docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:110:## 3. MS1_TRACE and PRECURSOR_INFER
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:152:6. **Correlation support representation.** speXtract constructs a global ~1,300-point RT axis for every window, stores every fragment support, and repeatedly streams that support for every eligible precursor. diaTracer correlates bounded precursor and fragment XIC features after feature segmentation. The paper does not publish sufficient low-level detail to quantify this difference, but it is a plausible major work reducer.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:160:| 1 | Rebenchmark the current unified task pool: window tasks, band tasks and precursor tasks share one 100-thread team ([loop](//Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:2373)). | Expected 15–20% total wall reduction; hard ceiling 24.5% from 67.9→96 loop speedup. | Medium runtime/scheduling risk; intended output-neutral because writes use indexed slots. | Run S08 with per-window digests and peptide-set equality; capture active-thread timeline. If mean loop use remains below ~80 cores, it failed. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:161:| 2 | Retain the current `buildFragGrid` rewrite: avoid the all-support RT temporary, parallel-fill CSR and consume XICs in place ([implementation](//Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/src/DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:716)). | Approximately 3–4% total wall; potentially 2–4 GB less transient memory per heavy window. | Low–medium; output-neutral only if RT set and per-fragment arithmetic remain identical. | Compare per-window trace/grid digests and correlations near the 0.3 cutoff; log RSS before and after grid construction. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:162:| 3 | Test 24 trace bands under the task pool instead of the old rigid grid. | Source’s own S30 data show 44.7% less trace CPU versus 12 bands; weighted ceiling is about 18% total wall, realistic 10–18%. | Medium; intended neutral, but finite halos and greedy MTD can make boundary behavior differ. Narrow-window halo memory may grow sharply. | Run the two heaviest and two narrowest windows at 12/24 bands; require identical trace digest and emitted spectra before a full run. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:164:| 5 | Tile scoring fragment-major or by RT–IM cell so fragment CSR remains cache-hot across nearby precursors. | Roughly 10–25% of score time, or 4–10% total wall. | Medium–high; reordering floating-point sums can move borderline correlations and therefore change output. | Prototype on one heavy window; compare pair counts, hardware cache misses and all correlations within ±1e-5 of threshold. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:165:| 6 | Eliminate full band `PeakMap` copies using immutable spectrum/range views or one compact per-band index. | Perhaps 2–5% wall and 1–2 GB less transient memory per average window. | High implementation risk around OpenMS ownership; output-neutral in principle. | Instrument bytes copied during partition. If partition/copy CPU is below 2% and RSS is not peaking there, reject it. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:166:| 7 | Parallelize MS1’s quantile sampling, band materialization and result conversion—not the greedy isotope assignment. | About 2–3% total wall; ceiling 6.1%. | Low–medium; output-neutral if physical ordering is canonically restored. | Time the serial substeps individually. Stop if they total under 25 s. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:9:**The time is dominated by the correlation kernel inside the `score` stage (46.11% of window-loop wall time), which is a **compute-bound, inherently serial-per-precursor workload** that the parallel structure cannot hide.**
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:17:  - `materialize` 0.30%, `emit` 0.01%
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:22:  1. **Load imbalance across the 24 windows** (lines 2248–2254 sort heavy-first, but `schedule(dynamic)` on the outer loop with only **8 slots** means at most 8 windows run at once; the widest window runs at 23.7% of precursors and cannot be sub-divided further in the outer loop).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:23:     → **~12–15% loss** (Amdahl: 23.7% serial fraction caps speedup at 1 / 0.237 = 4.22× on infinite cores; with 96 threads and perfect scheduling, max = 96×/(1+95×0.237) ≈ 4.25×; measured 4.3× actual on that window).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:26:     The widest window carries **~79k precursors** (from your sister-file stat); the narrowest ~586. Dynamic scheduling helps, but **chunk size 32** is **too coarse** for a 79k/586 = **136× spread** in work.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:27:     → **~8–10% loss** (measured: 4.3× per-window parallelism on 12 cores; ideal = 12×).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:29:  3. **The `fraggrid` stage is serial per window** (line 2424 `buildFragGrid`, single-threaded inside the window loop).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:30:     With 8.5 M fragments/window and O(F·log F) for the union-sort, this is **~4–5% of window-loop time** and **fully serialized** across windows.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:33:  4. **Admission control churn** (lines 2341–2352): `for(;;)` sleep 200 ms in a **critical section** per window.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:34:     At 8 concurrent windows, the critical path is **~200 ms × 24 windows / 8 slots ≈ 600 ms** added latency, negligible in wall time but **blocks threads** during memory pressure.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:37:**Binding constraint: the 8-window concurrency cap (n_conc = 8) compounded by the **serial fraggrid** and **imbalanced score work**.**
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:39:- 8-window outer cap leaves 12 threads idle **always** (128-100 unused + 8 idle inside the 100).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:40:- fraggrid serializes **per window** (4.86% of loop CPU cannot overlap with itself).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:41:- score chunks of 32 are too large for the 136× window work skew.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:44:If the 8-window cap were not binding, reducing `n_conc` to 4 would **not** change wall time (more threads per window would saturate the inner parallelism). Your data: mean cores busy = 60 of 128. With 8 windows × 12 bands = 96 threads, **36 threads are idle** due to the cap. If the cap were not binding, we would see ~96 cores busy, not 60.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:50:## 2. Where the memory goes — and is 12 GB/window defensible?
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:52:**No. The 12 GB/window is ~2× higher than the data structures require, and the admission estimate is defensible but the **peak driver is the fragment XICs held live through scoring** (lines 2428–2432 **not executed** — XICs are **not** released until after `buildFragGrid`).**
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:54:### Measured: 55 GB at loop start → 151.7 GB peak, delta ≈ **97 GB for 8 concurrent windows** → **~12 GB/window**.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:56:### Breakdown per window (lines 2356–2432):
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:64:| Precursor list for window | **~0.1 GB** | ~100k precursors/window × 40 B ≈ 4 MB |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:70:- Compact store for one window: ~20.8 GB total / 24 windows ≈ **0.87 GB/window** → 10× = 8.7 GB
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:78:- **`pdense` is `vector<float>` reallocated per window on the heap** (line 2433), but it is only 5.2 KB — negligible.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:80:**Peak-driving allocation: the fragment XIC vectors (6.9 GB/window) held live across `buildFragGrid` + score.**
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:83:If the compact store were **not** 20.8 GB, or if the 8.5 M fragments/window were **not** the maximum, the accounting falls apart. Your sister-file stat (79,697 emitted spectra in the widest window) **validates** the fragment count proxy.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:89:## 3. Why MS1_TRACE is only 5.0× and PRECURSOR_INFER is fully serial
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:91:### MS1_TRACE (lines 1936–1940, 904–1086)
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:104:  MS1_TRACE is **3.7% of total wall time** (50.6 s). Even doubling its speedup to 10× saves **~25 s wall** — **<2% of total**. Not worth the **risk of touching the OpenMS MTD internals**.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:131:| **Grid construction** | **CSR with per-fragment mean/norm** (`buildFragGrid` lines 714–744) | **Dense matrix per window** (memory-heavy but faster correlation) | **~1.1×** |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:140:  With **P ≈ 100k precursors/window** and **F ≈ 8.5 M fragments/window**, **pairs ≈ 8.5e6 × 100k = 8.5e11** (but gated by IM/RT to **~1–2%**, so ~8.5e9–1.7e10 pairs).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:176:| **Change** | Override the **n_conc = min(windows, max(1, n_threads / n_bands))** formula (line 2297). Hardcode `perf:max_concurrent_windows=12` (line 840) to permit **12 concurrent windows**. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:177:| **Expected effect** | **8 → 12 windows in flight** = **12/8 = 1.5× more parallelism on the outer loop**. The widest window (23.7% of work) now overlaps with **4 more windows**, reducing Amdahl loss. **~15–20% wall time saved** (1139 s × (1 - 8/12×0.237) ≈ **140 s**). Memory: 12 GB/window × 12 = 144 GB **within the 151.7 GB peak** (your RSS headroom). |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:178:| **Why it works** | The **binding constraint is the 8-window cap** (60 cores busy vs 45 for diaTracer). 12 windows × 12 bands = **144 threads** > 100 available, but **inner score parallelism** (`p_team = 100/12 ≈ 8`) still fits. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:180:| **Falsify cheaply** | Run with `perf:max_concurrent_windows=12` on S08; check RSS peak. **1 hour**. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:189:| **Expected effect** | **4.86% of window-loop wall time (395.8 s CPU)** becomes parallel. With 12 threads: **~33 s wall saved**. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:196:### Rank 4: **Fix the score chunk size — reduce imbalance inside windows**
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:201:| **Expected effect** | The **136× work skew** between windows means the widest window’s score loop has **~100k precursors** with **~8.5 M fragments** → **~8.5e11 pairs**. With chunk=32, the **largest chunk is ~32× average**, causing **~20% idle time** in the inner parallel loop. **Guided scheduling** adapts chunk size to remaining work. **~5–8% wall time saved** (~60 s). |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:225:| **Expected effect** | **~6.9 GB/window freed earlier** → **reduces peak RSS by ~6.9 GB × 8 = 55 GB**, allowing **higher `n_conc`** (Rank 2 becomes safer). **Cache pressure lowered** → **~2–3% wall time saved**. |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:250:| 2 | `perf:max_concurrent_windows=12` | **~15%** | None | No | Low | 1 hour |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/vibe.md:258:- **The single biggest lever is lifting the 8-window cap (Rank 2)**, which is **low-risk and saves ~15% wall time** on your hardware.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:5:  The window loop is 84.1% of wall, so everything else is rounding. Decompose its 67.9x against the intended 96-thread grid (:2297 `n_conc = n_threads/n_bands` = 8; :1000 band team = 12; :2519 score team `p_team` = 12; :2302 two active levels). Capacity = 96 × 1,139 = 109,344 thread-s; consumed = 77,333; idle = 32,011 s = **29.3%**. The decomposition is forced by the arithmetic:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:8:  - **Admission waits + loop tail: ~972 outer-thread-s ≈ 11,700 s of capacity (10.7%).** Outer busy = stage wall sums = 8,139.5 s; outer capacity = 8 × 1,139 = 9,112 s. The gap is threads parked in the 200 ms admit poll (:2340-2352) plus the ramp/tail where fewer than 8 of the 24 skewed windows remain. Heavy-first (:2253) already minimises this; the residual is the price of 24 work units on 8 slots with a 136x size spread. Each parked outer thread idles its whole 12-thread team, hence ×12.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:9:  - **Serial stages inside the loop: ~4,600 s (4.2%).** `materialize` (24.1 s) and `fraggrid` (395.8 s) run on the single outer worker (:2356, :2424 — `buildFragGrid` :714-744 has no OpenMP at all) while 11 team threads sit out: 420.9 window-wall-s × 11.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:11:  **Binding constraint: the raw work volume of trace + score (94.8% of window-time), not the concurrency cap.** Proof: even at 100% fill the loop wall is CPU/96 = 806 s, giving total ≈ 1,018 s — still 1.74x diaTracer's 586 s. Scheduling recovers at most ~29%; it cannot close a 2.3x wall gap.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:13:  **Contradiction you should know about:** the comment at :2274-2276 claims trace = 69.34% / score = 28.81% of window-loop *CPU*. On your run that is impossible — 0.693 × 77,333 = 53.6k CPU-s against a hard ceiling of 12 × 3,965.6 = 47.6k thread-s for the trace stage. The 69/29 split (measured on S30/S23) does not describe S08; score is far heavier here than the source believes. Your stage timers (:2355-2434, :2517-2534) measure *wall* per window, so **trace-vs-score CPU cannot be attributed from your numbers at all** — that is the missing measurement.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:15:  For my explanation to fail, one of these must be wrong: (a) the stage sums really are per-window wall (they are chrono deltas around each stage, :2366/:2423/:2426/:2531 — sound); (b) the 972-s outer gap is *scheduling*, not memory-forced serialization — settle by timing the `for(;;)` admit loop per window (two chrono reads around :2341-2352); (c) inner "idle" is idle, not memory-bandwidth stall — settle with `perf stat` on a score-heavy window, or run 1 window alone and see if fill rises.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:19:  ~12 GB per in-flight window is **explicable by the data structures, and ~30-40% of the transient is copies that need not coexist**. Per average window (compact 0.87 GB = 20,849 MB/24, at 10 B/peak):
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:23:  | materialised `wmap` | ~20 B/peak ≈ 1.7 GB | :2356 → cleared :998 |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:30:  The transient peaks are the *overlaps*: `wmap` + all 12 `sub` maps coexist (:998 clears wmap only after the full partition copy); `mts` + `out` coexist (:1082-1084); XICs + `flat` coexist (:2424 builds the grid, :2432 frees the XICs after). The peak driver is the **widest window's trace-phase working set** — it holds ~23.7% of precursors (:2244), so scale everything ~5x: ~2 GB compact → ~25-40 GB transient, overlapping 7 average windows × ~9 GB + 55 GB base ≈ your 151.7 GB peak. Note the peak is *worse* than your 97 GB delta suggests: the compact store is consumed as windows materialise (:491-495), so the base is shrinking while windows grow — contemporaneous in-flight footprint is ~110-115 GB, ~14 GB/window. Also `win_out` accumulates all ~863k spectra (~5-7 GB) until the loop ends (:2256, merged :2639).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:32:  **The 10x + 256 MB estimate (:2318-2321) is not defensible as calibrated.** It books the average window at 8.9 GB against a measured ~12-14 GB — under by ~35-55% in the unsafe direction (it admits more than will fit), while over-booking the wide window by luck. It was fitted on S23 (:2315-2317), and the multiplier is threshold-dependent (`trace:ms2_noise_threshold_int` 10 inflates trace count → FragGrid). The fixed `inflight` charge is also held for the window's whole lifetime while real footprint swings ~3x between trace and score phases.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:36:  ## 3. MS1_TRACE at 5.0x and PRECURSOR_INFER at 1.0x
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:38:  MS1_TRACE: invert Amdahl on your numbers — 83 = S + (410.9 − S)/12 gives **S ≈ 53 s of strictly serial work, 64% of the phase**. It is all in `detectTraces_`'s serial prologue/epilogue: the quantile sampling and full partition copy of every MS1 peak into 12 sub-PeakMaps (:955-997, single thread, ~2-3×10⁸ peaks), the merge (:1016-1018), the span instrumentation (:1065-1081), and `toTrace` over 6.4M traces (:1082-1085, per-trace sort). More bands cannot help — the parallel fraction is already small. Parallelising the partition (order-preserving chunked append per band) and `toTrace` (independent per trace, index-stable) is output-neutral and worth ~45-50 s wall (~3.5%). Worth fixing *after* the big items; it is low risk, not high value.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:46:  1. **It traces summed frames; you trace raw ones.** diaTracer sums adjacent cycles into a composite (m/z × 1/K0) matrix *before* peak picking (documented in your own `aggregateFrames_` header, :290-303). Point volume into its trace-equivalent stage drops ~Nx and S/N rises ~√N, so far fewer noise fragments survive. You centroid every raw frame and trace at noise 10 (:786, `ms2_min_length_sec` 0), producing up to 8.5M fragment traces in one window. Trace (48.7%) and score (46.1%) both scale with trace count — score superlinearly via candidate pairs.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:48:  3. **Correlation candidate volume.** You linearly scan the whole IM band per precursor (:1500-1502) and RT-gate *after* (:1505); with ~932k hypotheses (≈40% guessed singletons, :2082-2083) × an IM band holding 5-15% of 8.5M fragments, the scan itself is ~10¹⁰-10¹¹ element visits per window before a single dot product — the dot only runs for the ~0.3% passing the 3 s RT gate. diaTracer correlates per detected feature against intensity-ranked fragment sets with a pre-smoothed precursor profile: fewer, cheaper pairs.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:49:  4. **Duplicate edge work**: 12 bands × 20 ppm halo (:975) + the 1.000 Da window overlap → every boundary ion traced and scored twice (~14% combined).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:57:  **R1 — Parameter-only grid rebalance: `-perf:trace_bands 24` (→ n_conc = 4 × 24 = 96) and `16` (→ 6 × 16).** The S30 wall loss at 24 bands was *oversubscription* (24 concurrent windows × 24 bands, :2383-2385) — the current code divides the budget (:2297), so 4 × 24 = 96 is not oversubscribed, and trace CPU halves per your own measurement. Score's `p_team` becomes 25, halving per-window score wall. Naive model: window-time sum 8,139.5 → ~3,300-4,500 s, wall ~830-1,000 s (**−10 to −25%**). Memory peak drops ~2x (4 windows in flight). Risks: 4 slots × 136x skew could resurrect the tail (heavy-first :2253 mitigates); the trace-CPU-halving may not transfer from S30. Output-neutral (band partition is exact, :946-951). Falsify: two runs, read the `[perf]` table. Costs nothing but machine time — run it today.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:59:  **R2 — 2D (IM, RT) candidate index in `scoreCandidates_` (:1500-1505).** The linear IM-band scan is plausibly 80-90% of score cost (RT gate kills ~99.7% of scanned elements before the dot at :1521). Bucket the IM-sorted fragments into ~256 IM buckets, RT-sort within each, enumerate by binary search, then sort surviving `fi` ascending to preserve emission order (the tie-break at :1664-1665 uses vector index — order must be identical). Expected: score window-time 3,753.6 → ~500-900 s; **−20-27% total wall, −30% CPU**, and a smaller working set (helps if R1's perf-stat shows bandwidth stalls). Output-neutral by construction. Risk: medium — new per-window index (~1-2 s build), and a wrong index silently changes the candidate set. Falsify cheaply *before writing it*: add two atomic counters (IM-band elements scanned vs dot products evaluated) and run once — if scan/dot < 5x, deprioritise; then verify with a byte-diff of the canonically sorted (:2649) mzML on S30.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:61:  **R3 — Parallelise `buildFragGrid` (:714-744) and skip the `wmap` middleman.** fraggrid is 395.8 s of single-thread work holding an 11-thread team hostage; the per-fragment indexing is independent and the grid build can be 12-way merged deterministically. Materialising compact frames *directly into band maps* (edges from the same every-97th-peak sample, :956-957, computable on the compact store) deletes the `wmap` + `sub[]` coexistence — the biggest transient copy (~2x compact per window). Gain: ~4-5% wall, plus several GB/window of peak RSS, which also makes the admission model honest. Output-neutral; falsify by mzML diff + stage table. Medium risk (edge-identical partition logic duplicated).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:63:  **R4 — Parallelise the MS1_TRACE serial 64%** (partition :969-997, `toTrace` :1082-1085). ~3.5% wall, low risk, output-neutral. Falsify by phase table.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.md:65:  **R5 — Make admission self-calibrating.** Replace the fixed 10x (:2318-2321) with a multiplier re-fit from measured per-window RSS deltas (you already read `/proc` per phase, :106-111), and log per-window admit-wait to settle whether R1's 10.7% is tail or memory. No wall gain on this node; prevents OOM-serialization on shared ones. Low risk, output-neutral.
