docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:256:  And GOMP spin: with OMP_WAIT_POLICY unset, libgomp spins GOMP_SPINCOUNT (~300k) before sleeping — at 8×12 nested teams forming/completing thousands of times (24 windows × 2 inner regions + EPD regions + band loops: ~24×3=72 region entries, negligible). Barrier waits within imbalanced regions: threads sleep after ~1 ms spinning — negligible CPU. OK, not a major factor; mention only briefly? Skip or one line. Actually inner idle 15.7k s is mostly genuine imbalance (waiting at barrier, sleeping), not spin. Fine.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:258:  Also `schedule(dynamic)` on band loop at :1000 with 12 bands over 12 threads — one band = one chunk; if one band is 3× the median, the trace stage wall = that band. Quantile bands equalize PEAK COUNT, not trace work; work per peak varies with local intensity/noise. Suggest: more bands than threads per window for the band loop? bands=12 with num_threads(12) → dynamic can't rebalance. Could do 24 sub-bands over 12 threads (2 chunks each) → better balance but more halo. Or make score-style chunking. Minor vs grid A/B.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:262:  Now Q1 "which of my numbers would have to be wrong for your explanation to fail": My decomposition rests on: stage wall sums (8,139.5) ≈ outer-busy time; CPU 77,333; wall 1,139. If stage sums were actually CPU-s (mislabeled) or if admit-waits are large (memory-bound rather than scheduling), the conclusion "inner imbalance 14.4% + tail/admission 10.7% + serial 4.2%" shifts. Specifically: if admit-wait ≈ 0 (measurable), then the 972 s outer gap is pure tail/heavy-first granularity → window rebalance won't help, only smaller work units. If per-band timings show balance, then inner idle is memory-bandwidth stalls → the score index/footprint fixes matter more than scheduling. Ask for: per-window admit-wait, per-band wall, per-stage CPU.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:265:  1. **Input-volume to the tracer**: diaTracer sums adjacent cycles pre-centroid (S/N ↑ ~√N, point count ↓ ~N×) so its peak-picking/trace stage sees ~1/5 the points and far fewer noise traces. speXtract traces every raw frame at noise 10 → 2.19e9 peaks compacted, 8.5M traces in big windows. Trace+score are ~95% of window CPU; both scale (super)linearly with trace count. This alone can be ~2-3×.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:268:  4. **Redundant halo work**: 12 bands × 20 ppm halo + 1 Da window overlap → duplicated tracing at every edge (~12% + ~2%).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:269:  5. Valley-splitting EPD over 8.5M traces/window (+38% wall documented) — diaTracer doesn't split OpenMS-style traces because it doesn't build them.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:275:  Q3 deeper: why 5.0x on MS1_TRACE despite 12 bands:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:293:  R4. Parallelise buildFragGrid + drop the wmap middleman (materialise compact→bands directly) — -3-5% wall, big transient-memory cut (trace-phase footprint ~2× less); output-neutral; medium risk (partition must produce identical edges/content — diffable).
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:295:  R5. MS1_TRACE partition/toTrace parallelisation — -2-3% wall; output-neutral; low risk.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:297:  R6. Admission self-calibration (measure actual per-window RSS delta; multiplier 10→~14 or adaptive) — robustness; tiny wall effect here; prevents OOM-driven serialization elsewhere; add admit-wait timing to settle whether item-2 losses are tail or memory.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:303:  Also: memory peak driver statement: the peak-driving allocation is most plausibly the widest window's (FragGrid.flat + still-live Trace XICs + sub[] band copies + MTD trace store) transient — during buildFragGrid, xic (16 B/pt) and flat (8 B/pt) coexist: 24 B/pt; if the wide window has ~4e8-4e9 support points → 10-90 GB. Given measured +97 GB total, and the compact store for the wide window ~5 GB, a 24 B/pt structure with 5 GB×(1 pt per ~10 B... compact peaks ≈ support points lower bound... support points ≤ peaks. Wide window peaks ≈ 5e8 (5 GB compact at 10 B) → XIC 8 GB + flat 4 GB + wmap 10 GB + sub 10 GB... these don't all coexist: wmap freed at :998 (before MTD completes? map.clear(true) at :998 happens right after partition, BEFORE band tracing — so during band tracing: sub[] (12 maps, ~10 GB incl halo) + growing MTD traces. After: traces+XICs (~16+ GB), then +flat (~8 GB) while xic alive. Peak per wide window ~25-35 GB. So peak total 151.7 = 55 base + ~7 avg windows × 9-10 + wide-window transient ~30 ≈ 150. ✓ Consistent. So the peak driver = widest window's trace-phase working set (sub copies + MTD traces + XIC + grid). Good.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:305:  Is 10× + 256 MB defensible? For the wide window: need = 50 GB vs actual transient ~30 GB → over-covered; for average window: need 8.9 vs actual ~10-12 → under-covered. It's a crude linear model; direction fine, calibration ±40%. The bigger issue: `inflight` is charged `need` for the WHOLE window lifetime, but actual footprint swings ~4× between phases; and admission re-reads MemAvailable which includes page cache — on a 128-core shared node with big page cache, MemAvailable is optimistic. It's defensible as a heuristic but not as a guarantee; the measured 97 GB delta vs 8×8.9=71 GB booked proves the booking under-covers by ~35% in practice. Recommend self-calibrating multiplier (measure RSS delta per window, adapt) — cheap, keeps shared-node safety.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:307:  One more check on the emit path: pslot pre-sized per precursor (:2518) — 39k MSSpectrum objects per window ~ fine.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:315:  And "windows processed heaviest-first" — :2253 stable_sort by wbytes desc, schedule(dynamic) → first 8 iterations start immediately (the 8 biggest). ✓.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:317:  And admission: before materialise, for(;;) with 200 ms sleep inside critical — confirmed :2340-2352. Note: the sleep is OUTSIDE the critical (breaks out, sleeps) ✓. Note subtle: `need` computed from `project()` = 10× + 256 MB ✓ :2318-2321.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:322:  1. Band partition copies all peaks into sub[] (:969-997) instead of materialising compact→bands directly (compact already partitioned by m/z quantiles possible). Double transient: wmap + sub coexist briefly... actually sub[b] accumulates while wmap fully alive; then wmap cleared. Peak = wmap + all sub ≈ 2× window peaks.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:327:  So per-window transient inflation ~3-4× over minimal — that's where the 12 GB comes from. The admission model (10×) roughly prices this.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:333:  Also small: `schedule(dynamic)` outer loop with admission INSIDE the iteration means a thread holding a big window blocks others from... no, dynamic gives each thread next window when free; admission may make a thread wait while others could take smaller windows that fit — head-of-line blocking: a thread stuck waiting for a 50 GB need blocks its slot even though a later small window would fit. With heavy-first + dynamic, iteration order is fixed (heavy→light); waiting threads each hold a specific wi. Minor: could re-order to "admit any window that fits" (work-stealing by size). Small gain; mention under R6.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:357:  - materializeWindow :467-497; frees frames :491-495
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:365:  - window overlap 1 Da — from user.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:376:  One more check on buildFragGrid cost driver: the union-grid build pushes EVERY xic point into g.rt then sorts (F×S log) — 8.5M×50 = 4e8 doubles sort per big window, single thread: ~10-20 s. Plus per-point lower_bound log(G). fraggrid 395.8 s total window-wall across 24 windows ≈ 16.5 s avg — consistent with serial sort+index. Parallelise: per-thread partial vectors + k-way merge, or construct grid from frame RTs directly! Actually the grid is the union of fragment RTs — but every fragment XIC point comes from a frame RT of that window; the union of xic RTs ⊆ window frame RTs. g.rt could just be the sorted unique frame RTs of the window (known before tracing, from CompactFrame.rt!) — no giant sort needed; deterministic; and lower_bound per point still needed but could use frame index directly... The grid = union of fragment RTs — equal to frame RTs if every frame contributed ≥1 fragment, else a subset. Using ALL frame RTs as grid would add empty grid points → changes G → changes Pearson (variance over full grid G!) → OUTPUT CHANGE. So must keep union-of-fragment-RTs semantics. Can still build faster: collect per-fragment sorted xic RTs (each sorted), k-way merge via... or parallel partition by RT range: each thread builds sorted sub-vector for its fragment chunk, then merge 12 sorted vectors (parallel pairwise merge). Deterministic. Fine — details for the implementation; keep the recommendation.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:378:  Also for the score index: note frag_im sorted (:2417-2418, fragments sorted by im then mz then rt). The scan lo..hi over IM band then RT-gates. Build: bucket the IM-sorted array into blocks (e.g., 1024 fragments), keep per-block min/max RT → skip blocks whose RT range doesn't overlap [pc.rt-3, pc.rt+3]? RT within block unsorted (sorted by mz within same im? sort key is (im,mz,rt,intensity) — within an IM band block, RT effectively unsorted). Block min/max precompute: cheap, exact (no false negatives), skips blocks fully outside RT window. With 3 s window over ~30 min gradient, ~99.7% blocks skipped → scan cost ~300×... but blocks spanning 1024 fragments across diverse RTs will have wide min/max... fragments within a tight IM slice come from all RTs — block min/max RT ≈ full range → no skipping. Better: within coarse IM buckets (say 256 buckets over the window's IM range), sort fragments by RT → two binary searches per bucket. Enumerate candidates bucket-by-bucket; to keep global fi-ascending order for tie-break safety, collect candidate fis then sort them (candidates per precursor small after RT gate, ~thousands) — sort ascending fi → identical emission order. Index build: F log F per window (~8.5M×log ≈ 200M ops ≈ 0.2 s×12 threads parallelisable, or just do it single-threaded ~1-2 s vs 156 s avg score window-wall). Fine.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:380:  Expected gain quantification for R1: score window-wall 3,753.6 s. Scan share est: scan visits per precursor ≈ |IM band|. Assume scan ≈ 80-90% of score CPU (RT gate kills ~99%+ before dot). New score ≈ 0.1-0.2× old + index build. Score wall-sum → ~400-800 s. Window-loop wall → from 1,139 to ~750-800 s (sum/8: (24.1+3965.6+395.8+600+1)/8 ≈ 623 + fill losses... hmm sum becomes ~4,987/8 = 623 s + same idle structure ≈ 700-790 s). Total wall → ~970-1,060. That's -22-28%. And CPU 77.3k → ~47-52k (-35%). Then mean cores busy drops; diaTracer gap narrows to ~1.8× CPU.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:390:  Let me also answer "which of my numbers would have to be wrong": The 29%-idle decomposition fails if (i) stage wall sums are wrong (timers measure something else — but they're chrono deltas around each stage per window, code :2355-2434,2517-2534 — sound); (ii) the 77,333 CPU includes massive spin (then inner "idle" isn't recoverable); (iii) admission waits ≈ 972 outer-s are memory-forced not scheduling (then raising concurrency OOMs). Each has a cheap measurement. Also if "60 of 128 mean" is right (it checks out arithmetically), the ceiling claim stands: even perfect packing gives loop wall ≈ CPU/96 = 806 s → total ~1,020 s ≈ still 1.74× diaTracer wall. So scheduling alone can't reach parity — work must shrink. That requires only CPU=77,333 and threads=96 to be right.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:396:  Also mention: the 4 idle threads (100-96) — could set n_bands... 100/12=8.33→8; could use n_conc=8, bands=12, +LOAD uses 100. Or 9 windows × 11 bands = 99? n_conc=min(24, 100/11=9), bands 11 → 99 threads. Marginal (+3%). Not worth vs R2.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:400:  Base 55 GB: compact 20.9 + ms1_map during MS1 trace... at loop start 55 GB includes compact 20.9 GB; the rest ~34 GB = ms1_traces (6.4M × xic ~ 16 B×~20 pts + 64 ≈ 2.5 GB) + precursors + PeakMap overhead of... hmm 34 GB unexplained. MS1 picked map: MS1 = 1,343 frames × ~690k RAW peaks; after PeakPickerIM centroiding much less, say 100-200k/frame → 1.5-2.7e8 peaks × 20 B ≈ 3-5.4 GB + FloatDataArray... Let me not over-derive; base is measured 55 GB and mostly freed/consumed through loop (compact consumed per window). Actually — the compact store is consumed as windows materialise (:491-495), so base SHRINKS during loop while windows add. Peak 151.7 with base shrinking means in-flight peak is even higher than 97 GB relative to contemporaneous base. Note this: late in the loop, compact store is mostly freed (say half → -10 GB), so in-flight windows at peak moment ≈ 97 + up to ~10-20 GB of still-unconsumed compact ≈ 110-115 GB → ~14 GB/window. Slightly strengthens "admission under-covers".
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:404:  End RSS 87,886 MB after loop: compact freed, windows freed, but all_out 7 GB + ms1_traces + base stuff. 87.9 GB residual at SORT — that includes... whatever; peak was 151.7 mid-loop.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:406:  OK. Also one more structural observation for Q1: `schedule(dynamic)` + admission inside iteration + heavy-first: the 8 threads grab the 8 biggest windows first; these also have the largest `need`. If budget only fits, say, 6 of them, two threads SLEEP while... they can't take smaller windows (dynamic assignment already made at iteration start? With schedule(dynamic), chunks assigned on demand — thread requests next iteration when free. The assignment order is wi order. A thread stuck in admit loop for window 9 holds its assignment; another finishing thread takes window 10 (smaller, may fit). So smaller windows CAN pass a blocked big one — good, no head-of-line blocking beyond one slot. OK fine.)
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:410:  Let me also double-check user's stage table claim "executed at most 8 windows at a time" — but the sum 8,139.5 includes score executed with 12 threads per window; window-wall per window is the window's own elapsed; 8 concurrent → sum/8 ≤ wall ✓ 1,017 ≤ 1,139.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:412:  Now — answer "Why is MS1_TRACE only 5.0x when banded 12 ways": serial partition (:956-997) + serial toTrace (:1082-1085) + serial merge/spans + band imbalance; also MTD MS1 apex list is huge (6.4M traces) and the band with the densest low-m/z region dominates. CPU 410.9 in 83 s → 5.0x. Estimate serial fraction: if 12x-parallel portion were perfect, wall = serial + par/12. 83 = S + (410.9 - S)/12 → S = (83×12 - 410.9)/11 = 53.1 s serial ≈ 64% of the phase is serial! Great number to quote: Amdahl inversion gives ~53 s of one-thread work inside MS1_TRACE (partition copies ~2-3e8 peaks + toTrace 6.4M traces + sorts). So the fix is not more bands; it's parallelising the copies. Worth ~45-50 s wall (3.5%) — cheap, output-neutral, do it after R1/R2.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:416:  Q2 numbers: per-window footprint table (avg window compact 0.87 GB):
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:422:  - So a big window's trace-phase peak ≈ wmap 1.7 + sub 1.9 + traces ~2-4 + XIC 3-7 ≈ 9-14 GB; score-phase ≈ XIC freed but grid 3.5 + scalars 1 + pdense ≈ 5-6 GB. Average over phases × 8 ≈ 12 GB/window-time ✓ explicable WITHOUT invoking leaks. But: transient coexistence (wmap+sub; xic+flat; mts+out) means ~30-40% of the peak is copies that need not coexist. And the widest window (compact ~3-5 GB) scales all of this ~4-6× → 40-60 GB transient — that's the peak driver.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:424:  The 10×+256 MB estimate: prices the AVERAGE window at ~8.9 GB vs actual ~12 → under by ~35%; for the widest window need ~30-50 GB vs transient ~40-60 → roughly right by luck. As a safety mechanism on a shared node it's miscalibrated in the unsafe direction for average windows (books less than it uses → admits more than fit). Recommend measuring actual.
