 succeeded in 0ms:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-63-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-64-### The accounting problem:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-65-The design **double-counts** savings. The "no PeakMap materialisation" and "no double conversion" are the **same pass** (`materializeWindow`). The 30-50% estimate assumes these effects are additive, but they are not.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-66-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-67-### Is the gain mostly materialisation removal vs cache behaviour?
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-68-**It is mostly materialisation removal.** Here's why:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-69-- The materialisation pass is **O(N)** where N is all peaks in the window, and it **allocates and copies** every peak into OpenMS objects.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-70-- The detection pass (even with poor cache behaviour) still has to **read** every peak and perform the same search logic.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md:71:- If materialisation were cheap, the detection would still be 90.9% of the time. The fact that scoring (which also reads arrays) dropped from 47% to 4.8% when switching to parallel arrays proves that **layout matters**, but it does not prove that materialisation is the dominant cost.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-72-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-73-### How to tell the two apart with one measurement:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-74-Run trace detection on a window **with materialisation but with the compact store already in contiguous arrays**. If the time **does not drop significantly**, then the cost is in the extension search, not the layout. If it **does drop**, the layout is the bottleneck. This isolates the materialisation cost from the cache effect.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-75-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-76-### Falsifier assessment:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md:77:The 15% floor is **too low**. The materialisation pass alone should account for **at least 10-20%** of the 90.9% trace detection time (since it is a full pass over the same data). A 15% total reduction would mean the materialisation was **<15% of 90.9% ≈ 13.6% of window time**, i.e., **~500s on S30**. If you measure materialisation time directly and it is >600s, the 15% falsifier fires **even if the refactor is successful**. The falsifier should be **25%**, not 15%.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-78-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-79----
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-80-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-81-## 4. What will this break?
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-82-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-83-### Ranked risks (including missed ones):
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-84-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-85-| Rank | Risk | Symptom | Design coverage |
