docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-156-**Critical check at step 3:** If step 3 (exact semantics) does **not** produce the same peptide count as current, the whole refactor is **invalid** regardless of performance. Only if step 3 passes can you proceed to step 4's optimisations.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-157-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-158----
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-159-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-160-## Verdict
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-161-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-162-The refactor **is worth attempting**, but the current design **overstates faithfulness and understates risk**. The 3σ vs 1×ppm tolerance mismatch alone could make the results **incomparable**. The mobility-rank assumption **must be validated first** on real data. The 15% falsifier is **too weak**; raise it to 25%.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md-163-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/vibe.md:164:**Single measurement to settle whether the detection cost is in layout vs search:** Run the existing trace detection with `materializeWindow` **instrumented** to time only the materialisation pass. If this is <15% of the 90.9% trace detection time, the gain will come from cache behaviour during detection — which means the **integer comparison and contiguous layout** must deliver the full 30-50% for the refactor to succeed. If materialisation is >25% of trace detection time, the refactor is **almost certain** to succeed on time, but the **semantic differences** must still be resolved.
--
