docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-208-  Also mobility question: "when is it false": (1) picked/centroided data (this tool ALWAYS runs PeakPickerIM first — so distinct values are cluster centroids, not scans! The raw scans never reach the compact store). Wait — is that right? compactify runs after picker.pickIMCluster. So the claim "within one frame the distinct 1/K0 values ARE the scans" is false ON THIS TOOL'S OWN DATA unless the picker preserves per-scan values. What does PeakPickerIM do? It picks peaks along the IM dimension per m/z cluster, outputting one peak per IM cluster with the centroid 1/K0. The distinct 1/K0 values per frame after picking = number of distinct cluster centroids ≈ number of mobility-resolved features, NOT ~1000 scans. Hmm, but many features share... no, each cluster has its own centroid value, all distinct-ish. So a frame with 37k picked peaks could have up to 37k distinct 1/K0 values — MORE than the ~1000 scans! Then ranking gives rank ∈ [0, 37k), and uint16 scan field is fine, but "rank == TIMS scan" is simply false. The vendor scan index is unrecoverable from picked data. This is the strongest attack: the design's premise contradicts the tool's own pipeline (line 919/951: picker.pickIMCluster before compactify). Unless PeakPickerIM keeps the raw scan grid somehow (e.g., outputs the cluster's apex scan value, which would still be one of ~1000 distinct grid values IF the apex 1/K0 is reported at scan precision — the underlying values come from a calibration applied per scan, so apex values would be one of ~1000 distinct calibrated values, but calibrated per frame with frame-specific calibration → the distinct VALUES differ across frames even for the same scan!). Hmm: if picker reports the 1/K0 of the cluster's apex scan, computed from that frame's calibration, then within a frame there are ≤ ~1000 distinct values and ranking recovers scan order exactly IF apex values are strictly monotone in scan (they are, within a frame). Across frames the same scan yields slightly different calibrated values, but rank is per-frame so that's fine! So the premise hinges on WHAT PeakPickerIM emits as the 1/K0 of a picked cluster: apex-scan value (grid-aligned → rank == scan, modulo missing scans being skipped which doesn't matter for rank ordering... wait it does not matter: rank over distinct PRESENT values preserves order; two peaks from the same scan get same value → same rank; two peaks from different scans get different values → correct order. The rank is not the scan NUMBER but an order-preserving renumbering — for a tolerance in "scans", rank differences UNDERCOUNT scan differences when scans are missing (empty scans collapse). scan_tol derived from the same ranked values self-consistently uses the same collapsed coordinates — mostly consistent!) vs intensity-weighted centroid (grid-free → rank is just a total order of arbitrary floats; "scan" is meaningless, and cross-frame rank instability appears).
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-209-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-210-  So the falsification check: per frame, count distinct imq values and check whether the count is bounded by the vendor scan count (from tdf Frames.NumScans) AND whether the distinct values are consistent with a per-frame monotone grid (e.g., check if the same set of scans recurs: compute the median spacing signature). Cheaper: instrument one frame — dump distinct imq count; if it's ≫ NumScans (or ≈ peak count), the premise is false on picked data. Also direct: read tdf scan count for a frame and compare. And check what PeakPickerIM sets as IM value — read PeakPickerIM source! It's in the local OpenMS checkout. Let me check quickly: src/openms/source/PROCESSING/CENTROIDING/PeakPickerIM.cpp — look for how it sets the output IM value (weighted mean vs apex).
