docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err:216:  For Q3 (estimate): trace detection 3,869 s of 4,256 s window-elapsed. Where does time go inside MassTraceDetection? Not the materialisation (that's a separate phase maybe counted in trace?). Actually "trace detection is 90.9% of window loop" — measured via phase stats: does the "trace" phase include materializeWindow? Need to check the phase attribution in the window loop. materializeWindow is called at line ~2928 inside worker; if the Phase timer wraps only detectTraces_, materialisation might be counted elsewhere. The design says gain sources: no materialisation (−1 to −2 GB memory — note this is a MEMORY estimate not time!), integer comparison (small), cache (claimed dominant), no double conversion (one pass over 1.2e9 peaks removed). Honest accounting: of the table's four rows, two are about the materialisation pass (memory + the removed conversion pass), one is small, and cache is asserted dominant without measurement. Where's the 30-50% number from? It's not derived — stated as a range with a falsifier. Attack: MassTraceDetection's actual hot loop is findBestPeak_: per extension step, MZBegin/MZEnd binary searches + linear scan over the m/z window reading FloatDataArray. Cost scales with trace count × mean trace length × candidates in window. The layout change (contiguous SoA vs Peak1D+FDA) affects the candidate scan: Peak1D is 16 B... wait they say 20 B/peak + object overhead; the scan touches mz (8 B in Peak1D) + im (4 B in FDA) — two arrays, stride 16 and 4. Integer: tof (4B) + scan (2B) contiguous-ish. Candidate windows are small (tens of peaks) — cache effects modest. The big win may be seeding order sort over all peaks (intensities contiguous either way) and the removal of per-spectrum MSSpectrum overhead and the materialisation pass itself. Also `used` bitset vs char vector.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-217-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err:218:  Key: one measurement to separate materialisation from detection: time materializeWindow separately (add a Phase around it) — or run the integer detector on the slab and compare per-phase: actually the cleanest: instrument the existing run with a phase timer around materializeWindow within the worker. If materialisation is, say, 30% of the trace phase, then layout gain in detection proper is what's left. Also note: is materialisation currently counted in the 90.9%? If the "trace" phase includes materialisation, then the 30-50% estimate may be mostly the removed pass. One measurement: wrap materializeWindow in its own Phase and rerun S30 (or a subset) — splits the 3,869 s into conversion vs detection. Alternatively perf record / sampling profile of the trace phase: samples in materializeWindow vs MassTraceDetection::run_. A single `perf record` run (or the Phase timer, which the codebase already has infrastructure for) settles it.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-219-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-220-  Is the 15% falsifier the right floor? If materialisation is, say, 25% of the phase, then a pure-layout win below 15% means detection proper got slower even though the refactor "passed". The falsifier should be decomposed: materialisation removal sets a FLOOR of expected gain equal to the materialisation share (measurable beforehand!); the falsifier should be "detection proper (excluding the removed pass) does not fall by at least X%" — otherwise you can pass the falsifier on the pass removal alone while the new detector is actually slower per peak. Also 15% of trace detection = ~580 s on S30 — meaningful. But also should be judged on peptides, and runtime on equal peptide output. Suggest: pre-measure materialisation share M; expected gain from removal alone ≈ M; falsifier: total trace phase must fall by ≥ max(15%, M + 5%) or something; and report detection-proper time separately.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-221-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-222-  Also the docstring evidence ALREADY in the code (line 717-720): "4 bands 18:25 wall / 163 GB against 12 bands 12:53 / 103 GB — same work costs more when bands are bigger" — this says the cost is SUPERLINEAR in band size, i.e., in peaks per band. What scales superlinearly in MassTraceDetection? findBestPeak_ per extension step is O(log n + window). Number of extension steps ~ number of traces × length. Total peaks visited... The superlinearity with band size suggests something O(n log n) or worse per band: the chrom_apices sort (n log n, linearithmic, wouldn't explain 18:25 vs 12:53×3), or... the work_exp copy? select() per spectrum? Actually wait: 4 bands = each band 3× bigger than 12 bands; 18:25 vs 12:53 — going from 4→12 bands saved 30%. If cost were linear in band size, band count wouldn't matter (total work same). The superlinearity implies per-band quadratic-ish term: candidates in m/z window per extension scale with peak density... no wait, window is fixed ppm; density per band is the same regardless of band width. Hmm, what scales with band width: the number of live traces? MassTraceDetection doesn't keep live traces — it's apex-seeded sequential; cost = Σ over apices (extension steps × findBestPeak cost). findBestPeak cost ~ log(spectrum size) + window size — independent of band width. Sort of apices: n log n. Total n same... 4 bands: 4 sorts of n/4; 12 bands: 12 sorts of n/12 → 12-band sorting is CHEAPER (n log(n/12)). Not it either. Memory: 163 GB vs 103 GB — the 4-band run had bigger working set → more page faults/cache misses → slower per operation. That actually SUPPORTS the layout hypothesis (bigger working set = slower) — the materialised PeakMap per band is 3× bigger. So banding already proved the bandwidth sensitivity: same algorithm, same total peaks, 30% faster with 3× smaller working set. That's decent evidence FOR the cache hypothesis — should be cited in Q3 as the strongest existing evidence, and it also predicts the integer layout win is real but bounded: going from 20 B to 10 B is a 2× working-set cut, comparable to going from 4→8 bands, which was worth less than 30% (4→12 = 30% for 3×). So ~15-20% for 2× density — consistent with the low end of the estimate, plus the removed materialisation pass.
