docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-224-  Q4 (risks): rank the four + add missed ones:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-225-  - Listed: (1) min_sample_rate semantics — HIGH (and worse than stated: the tool runs OUTLIER mode, so reimplementing sample_rate termination is not a subtle difference, it's a different criterion); (2) seeding determinism — LOW for correctness (they added deterministic tie-break), but note OpenMS stable_sort ascending + reverse = ties in REVERSE original order; determinism per se is solved in the draft; the risk to peptides is low; (3) used marking parallelism — MEDIUM as stated (race across bands), but the draft uses per-call `used`, so the race is avoided; the REAL used-marking issue is semantic (invalid traces consume peaks) — that's not in their list at all; (4) mobility rank — HIGH (premise likely false on picked data).
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-226-  - Missed: (a) reestimate_mt_sd default true → OpenMS tolerance is 3× robust SD, not fixed ppm — the integer 1×ppm span is a different gate → different traces; symptom: trace count change, traces narrower/shorter, more fragmentation of isotope envelopes; detect by counting traces and length distribution vs OpenMS on one window. (b) invalid-trace peak consumption (the used-marking semantics) — symptom: missing traces around rejected blobs; the existing max_trace_length comment in the code (line 1258) already documents how OpenMS re-seeds rejected blobs — the integer version would emit NOTHING there permanently... wait no: OpenMS rejects over-length and re-seeds repeatedly, emitting nothing at that m/z but burning apexes? Actually the comment says peaks are marked visited ONLY for valid traces, so an over-length blob is "rejected, re-seeded by the next apex, and rejected again - emitting NOTHING at that m/z". So for over-length blobs OpenMS emits nothing after burning the whole apex list at that m/z?? Each re-seed re-extends and rejects again, until apices exhausted. Net: nothing emitted, all apices at that m/z consumed by the loop iteration (not by visited marking). Integer: marks used at extension → blob consumed once, nothing emitted — SAME output (nothing) at 1/N the cost. Hmm interesting — for max_len. But for min_len/sample-rate rejects, OpenMS leaves peaks free → other traces can take them as members; integer consumes them. E.g., a short trace rejected for min_len: OpenMS frees its peaks; a longer neighboring trace can absorb them. Integer: they're gone. Symptom: trace set differences in dense regions.
--
