docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-251-  Also for Q1 I should verify detectTraces_ params (outlier mode, reestimate default) by reading lines 1347-1500. And check the window-loop phases for Q3 (is materialization inside "trace" phase?). Read lines ~1380-1500 and 2900-3010 in new numbering. New numbering shifted +~32 lines vs old grep. Old grep lines: detectTraces_ at 1357 (pre-edit numbering from live grep — that grep was on the CURRENT file, so line numbers current: 1357 detectTraces_). Wait, the live grep returned current line numbers (detectTracesInteger_ at 738 vs my read at 745 — close but off by 7? grep said 738: `vector<Trace> detectTracesInteger_`... my read shows it at 745. Hmm, the grep ran BEFORE my second read — maybe the file is being edited WHILE I work?? git status shows modified. The grep output line 645 scan_tol matched my read at 645. detectTracesInteger_ grep at 738 vs read at 745 — discrepancy of 7 lines. Odd. Possibly the file changed between grep and read (user editing live). I should be careful and re-verify at the end. Actually the grep line 726 comment "detector peak for peak" vs my read line 725-726 — matches. And grep 738 "vector<Trace> detectTracesInteger_(PeakSlab& sl, double mass_ppm, double im_tol," vs read 745 — the signature changed between grep and read?? In the read, the signature has extra params (min_sample_rate, max_len_sec). So yes — the file was edited between my grep and my read. The user (or another agent) is actively editing. I'll work from the latest read and note the moving target. Let me re-grep quickly for detectTraces_ and read current state of the relevant regions. Also read PeakPickerIM IM value assignment.PeakPickerIM picks mobilogram traces with PeakPickerHiRes per m/z trace; the output IM value is `picked_trace[j].getMZ()` — the picked centroid of the mobilogram (a PeakPickerHiRes centroid, i.e., a weighted/refined centroid, NOT a raw scan value). So distinct 1/K0 values per frame after picking are cluster centroids — grid-free floats. The number of distinct values per frame ≈ number of picked features (can approach peak count, and can exceed the ~1000 TIMS scans). So the premise "distinct 1/K0 values ARE the scans" is FALSE for this pipeline's data: ranking recovers an ordinal of picked centroids, not the TIMS scan index. Rank order is still a valid total order of mobility (centroids are monotone in the underlying mobility), so as an IM GATE coordinate it mostly works — but it is not "the scan index", and per-frame scan_tol in rank units is a tolerance in "number of picked centroids", which varies with peak density, not with instrument scans. In a dense region, many centroids within 0.01 1/K0 → scan_tol huge → the gate accepts everything within 0.01 by rank-distance... wait no: scan_tol is computed as the max COUNT of distinct values within im_tol anywhere in the frame — dense regions make `worst` large, then the gate |rank diff| ≤ worst applies EVERYWHERE including sparse regions where worst ranks spans ≫ 0.01 1/K0. So the gate becomes density-dependent and can be dramatically wider than gate:delta_im in sparse regions. That's a serious correctness attack on §1. And the tail (uint16 scan overflow): distinct values ≤ 37k for MS2 frames — OK today, unguarded.
