docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-313-
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-314-  Q3 honest answer: The 30-50% is not derived from anything; the table lists four mechanisms, two of which (materialisation memory, double-conversion pass) are already measured separately by t_mat and are NOT in t_trace (materialize line is printed separately, and t_trace starts at _t2 after materialisation). So the "no materialisation / no double conversion" rows CANNOT contribute to reducing t_trace — they're already outside it! The only levers on t_trace are: cache/layout during detection, integer compare (small, design admits), and — crucially — the fact that the integer path as drafted SKIPS EPD (which is a chunk of t_trace; comment says EPD-off saved ~28% wall in the A/B). So: if detector=integer shows a 30% drop in t_trace as drafted, a large part is just skipping valley splitting — a correctness regression masquerading as a speedup. Honest estimate decomposition:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err:315:  - t_mat (separate line): removed, whatever it is — quote the S30 log's materialize line; that's the materialisation gain, already measured, and it's NOT part of the 3,869 s.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-316-  - EPD share of t_trace: must be retained → caps the gain. Measure: time do_split inside detectTraces_ (or read the existing A/B: 28:40 vs 20:46 suggests EPD ≈ 28% of total wall back then).
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err-317-  - Detector proper: the remaining share. The banding evidence (trace CPU 11,168 → 5,378 → 2,972 s across 5/12/24 bands, comment at line 2944-2948) shows detection CPU is strongly superlinear in band size — that's a candidate-scan complexity term (per-apex candidate list), NOT a layout term. Halving bytes/peak (20→10 B) is at most a ~2× bandwidth cut on a working set that already fits differently... The 4→12 bands gave 30% wall for a 3× working-set cut. A 2× density cut might give ~15-20% on the cache-bound part.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.err:318:  So my hostile verdict on the estimate: 30-50% off trace detection is optimistic for the detector proper; the number is reachable only by (a) counting the materialisation removal (already outside t_trace) or (b) silently dropping EPD. The honest expected gain on t_trace with EPD retained is likely 10-25%, dominated by banding-style working-set effects. The falsifier: 15% floor on t_trace is the wrong floor unless EPD is retained in the integer path AND materialisation stays excluded; specify: "t_trace with EPD included in both paths falls ≥15%". And the one measurement to separate layout from pass-removal: already exists — the materialize line (t_mat) vs trace line (t_trace) in the existing phase table; if the user wants detection-proper vs EPD, add one timer around do_split. Plus a perf record if they want cache-miss attribution. Actually the cleanest single measurement separating "removal of materialisation pass" from "cache during detection": t_mat is already that measurement — materialisation is NOT in the 90.9%. So the answer: the 30-50% cannot come from pass removal at all; it must come from detection; and the superlinearity evidence says detection cost is candidate-scan-driven, which layout alone doesn't fix — the draft's `pick()` DOES fix it (binary search + short walk instead of... wait, what does OpenMS actually do per extension? findBestPeak_: MZBegin/MZEnd binary searches + scan of the window — that's already binary search + short walk! The OpenMS path is NOT scanning whole spectra. Hmm — then why is detection superlinear in band size? The comment at 2949-2951 says "MassTraceDetection scans candidates per apex within the band's m/z range, so halving a band halves the candidate list for every apex" — that suggests findBestPeak_'s window scan covers many peaks... no, the window is ±3σ, small. What scales with band size: the apex list? For EACH apex, extension runs over ~all frames; per frame two binary searches. Total ≈ (#apices) × (trace length + misses) × (log n + window). #apices scales with peaks; per-band work should be ~linear in band peaks... unless: spectra per band contain only that band's peaks → smaller spectra → binary search cheaper (log) — marginal. The superlinearity measured (CPU 11,168→5,378 for 5→12 bands, i.e., 2.4× bands → 2.1× less CPU ⇒ work ∝ n^1.6-ish) — where from? Possibly: `work_exp` copy + select per spectrum (linear), the apex sort n log n (weak), OR cache: band PeakMap working set 5-band = 163 GB/... RSS 163 GB vs 103 GB — page-cache/memory-bandwidth thrash explains superlinear CPU via page faults? CPU time (utime+stime) includes stime — page faults count in stime! 163 GB RSS on the machine → memory pressure → stime inflates "CPU". That IS a layout/working-set effect, supporting the cache hypothesis. OK so evidence is mixed but points to working-set/bandwidth mattering a lot. I'll present this honestly: the strongest existing evidence that layout matters is the banding CPU scaling + RSS; the strongest evidence it's not just layout is that the algorithm's per-apex extension count doesn't change with layout. The single settling measurement: perf stat (LLC misses per peak) or simply: run the CURRENT code with bands=12 vs bands=24 on the same S30 — already done (2,972 vs 5,378 s CPU) — plus compare t_mat. Honestly, the decisive cheap measurement: time the three components on one S30 window set — materialise (have it), MTD proper, EPD — then compute the ceiling: gain ≤ MTD-proper share. I'll state that.
